700:
** ἀρέσκω** [ᾰ],
_imperfect_ ἤρεσκον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.37, etc.}}: _future_ ἀρέσω{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.33, _Epic dialect_ ἀρέσσω}} (συν-) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.901}}: _aorist_ ἤρεσα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.19, “Comica Adespota” 19.4 D}}., etc., _Epic dialect_ ἄρεσσα{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.301}}, _infinitive_ ἀρέσαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus}}: _perfect_ ἀρήρεκα{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 24, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.238}}:—_middle_, _future_ ἀρέσομαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 655 (Lyric poetry)}}, _Epic dialect_ ἀρέσσομαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.362:}}_aorist_ ἠρεσάμην, _Epic dialect_ ἀρ-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 255}}, _Epic dialect_ _participle_ ἀρεσσάμενος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.112, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 762}}: _aorist_ _passive_ in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 500}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἠρέσθην{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.13.8, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.9.6.}}
**I** of _person_ only, **make good, make amends,** ἂψ ἐθέλω ἀρέσαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.120}}:—_middle_, ταῦτα δ᾽ ὄπισθεν ἀρεσσόμεθ᾽ εἴ τι κακὸν νῦν εἴρηται{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.362, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.55, 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 4.377, 9.510}}; σπονδὰς θεοῖς ἀρέσασθαι **make full** drink-offerings to the gods, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus [prev. cited]}}
**I.2** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} also frequently in _middle_, with _accusative_ _person_ et _dative_ modi, **appease, conciliate,** αὐτὸν ἀρεσσάσθω ἐπέεσσι καὶ δώρῳ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.396}}; τὸν ξεῖνον ἀρέσσομαι ὡς σὺ κελεύεις{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 402; ὥς κέν μιν ἀρεσσάμενοι πεπίθωμεν δώροισι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.112, compare 19.179; καθαροῖσι βωμοῖς θεοὺς ἀρέσονται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 655; καί σε φίλως θυέεσσιν ἀρέσσατο 3rd c.BC: Maiistas Epicus 11}}: with _genitive_ _of things_, ἀρέσαντο φρένας αἵματος they **sated their** heart with blood, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 255.}}
**I.3** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, with _dative_ _person_, **please, satisfy,** οὔτε γάρ μοι Πολυκράτης ἤρεσκε δεσπόζων.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142; δεῖ μ᾽ ἀρέσκειν τοῖς κάτω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 75, compare 89}}; ἀεὶ δ᾽ ἀρέσκειν τοῖς κρατοῦσιν **to be obsequious to** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 93, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.11}}; ἀ. τρόποις τινός **conform to** his ways, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.19; τὸ κολακεύειν νῦν ἀρέσκειν ὄνομ᾽ ἔχει 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 42; πᾶσιν ἀρέσκω NT.1Cor.10.33; ἑαυτοῖς NT.Rom.15.1}}:—_middle_, μάλιστα ἠρέσκοντό [οἱ] οἱ ἀπ᾽ Ἀθηνέων **pleased** him most, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.128.}}
**II** of things, with _dative_ _person_, **please,** εἴ τοι ἀρέσκει τὰ ἐγὼ λέγω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.89; κάρτα οἱ ἤρεσε ἡ ὑποθήκη [prev. author] 8.58, compare 3.40, 6.22; τῷ τοῦτ᾽ ἤρεσε; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 409; σοὶ ταῦτ᾽ ἀρέσκει [prev. author] “Ant.” 211, etc.}}; τοῖς.. πρέσβεσιν ἤρεσκεν [the proposal] **pleased** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157d, +others: also in}}_aorist_ _passive_, μηδ᾽ ἀρεσθείη ποτέ (i.e. μηδὲν τῶν σῶν λόγων) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 500.}}
**III** with _accusative_ _person_, οὐ γάρ μ᾽ ἀρέσκει γλῶσσά σου τεθηγμένη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 584; οὐδέ σ᾽ ἀρέσκει τὸ παρόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 185 (Lyric poetry), compare “Or.” 210; τουτὶ.. μ᾽ οὐκ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 353, compare “Ach.” 189, “Ra.” 103, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.128; πότερός σε ὁ τρόπος ἀ.; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 433e, compare 391c, “R.” 557b, “Tht.” 172d}}: hence in _passive_, **to be pleased, satisfied,** οὐκ ἠρέσκετο λειπομένου Μαρδονίου ὑπὸ βασιλέος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.66}}; τῇ κρίσι **with** the decision, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.34; διαίτῃ Σκυθικῇ [prev. author] 4.78; τοῖς λόγοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.129, compare 2.68; τῇ σῇ συνουσίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 127b}}; later in _aorist_, ἠρέσθη τῇ γνώμῃ{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus [same place], +others; ἀρεσθεὶς τῷ πώματι 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta [prev. cited]}}
**IV** ἀρέσκει is used _impersonal_ to express **the opinion** or **resolution** of a public body, ταῦτα ἤρεσέ σφι ποιέειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.19; ἢν δ᾽ ἀρέσκῃ ταῦτ᾽ Ἀθηναίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1311}}; ἀρέσκει.. εἶναι Δελφῶν **it is resolved** that.., {{Refs|“SIG” 827 D 10}}; also of **prevailing opinions,** ἀρέσκει περὶ τρίψεως παραγγέλλοντας.. writers on massage **lay down the rule** that.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.96}}; τὰ ἀρέσκοντα the **dogmas** of philosophers, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.448a, 1006d, etc.}}:—_middle_, ἐξεῖναι παρ᾽ ὁποτέρους ἂν ἀρέσκηται ἐλθεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.35.}}
**V** _participle_ ἀρέσκων, ουσα, ον, **grateful, acceptable,** ὅσοις τάδ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἀρέσκονθ᾽{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 274; μηδὲν ἀρέσκον λέγειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.34; ἀρέσκοντας ὑμῖν λόγους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.5.}}
**V.2** of persons, **acceptable,** τὸν ἀρέσκοντα αὑτῷ προσλαμβάνειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 951e; τῇ πόλει ἀ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.13. (Cognate with ἀραρίσκω.)}}

703:
** ἀρετή** [ᾰ], ἡ,
**goodness, excellence,** of any kind, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} especially of **manly** qualities, ποδῶν ἀρετὴν ἀναφαίνων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.411; ἀμείνων παντοίας ἀρετὰς ἠμὲν πόδας ἠδὲ μάχεσθαι καὶ νόον 15.642}}; so of the gods, τῶν περ καὶ μείζων ἀ. τιμή τε βίη τε{{Refs|9.498}}; also of women, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.206}}; ἀ. εἵνεκα for **valour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.92: _plural_, ἀ. ἀπεδείκνυντο}} displayed **brave deeds,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.176, 9.40.}}
**b** later, of the gods, chiefly in _plural_, **glorious deeds, wonders, miracles**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|“SIG” 1172, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.17; ζῶσαι ἀ. “IG” 14.966, cf. NT.1Pet.2.9}}: also in _singular_, ὄψιν ἰδοῦσα ἀρετὴν τῆς θεοῦ{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 2.1426 b, compare 3rd c.BC: Isyllus Lyricus 62, “BSA” 21.169,180.}}
**2** generally, **excellence,** ἡ ἀ. τελείωσίς τις{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1021b20, compare “EN” 1106a15, etc.}}; of persons, ἄνδρα πὺξ ἀρετὰν εὑρόντα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.89, compare “P.” 4.187, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9.13, etc.; τὸ φρονεῖν ἀ. μεγίστη 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 112}}: in _plural_, **forms of excellence,** μυρίαι ἀνδρῶν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 13.8, compare 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 8, etc.; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a}}; especially **moral virtue,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 179, 263, +others, 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 6}}; opposed to κακία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.21, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 500d, “Lg.” 963a, 963cf, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.17, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1102a6, “Pol.” 1295a37, etc.}}; **good nature, kindness,** etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 163.}}
**2.b** of animals, things, as land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.198, 7.5, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2}}; ἡ ἐν ἀρετῇ κειμένη γῆ **productive** land, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 5.165 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἵππου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.88}}; κυνῶν, ἵππων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 335b; σκεύους [prev. work] 601d}}; [ἀστακοῦ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 24; ἀ. βίου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 618c; πολιτείας “Lg.” 886b, etc.}}
**3** **prosperity,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.45.}}
**II** ἀ. εἴς τινα **active merit, good service** done him, ἐς τοὺς Ἕλληνας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58, compare 2.40; ἀ. περί τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.8; ἀνταποδοῦναι ἀ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.19; ἀρετὰς παρασχέσθαι ὑπέρ τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.312}}; ἀρετῆς ἕνεκα, frequently in honorary Inscrr., {{Refs|“IG” 22.107.14, etc.}}
**III** **reward** of excellence, **distinction, fame,** πλούτῳ δ᾽ ἀρετὴ καὶ κῦδος ὀπηδεῖ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 313, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 80, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.53, +others; ἀθάνατος ἀ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1420, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 208d; ἃ ἆθλα τοῦ πολέμου τοῖς ἀνδράσιν ἐστίν, ἐλευθερία καὶ ἀ. 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 49}}; of God, δόξα καὶ ἀ.{{NT|NT.2Pet.1.3}}: in _plural_, **glories,** {{LXX+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 30, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.2, +others; πλοῦτος ἀρεταῖς δεδαιδαλμένος [prev. author] “O.” 2.53; γενναίων ἀ. πόνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 357 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.26; προγόνων ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 618b; in LXX}} frequently of the **praises** of God, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 42.8, +others}}
**IV** Ἀρετή personified, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Prodicus Philosophus 1, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 675, 3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 2, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2786, “SIG” 985.10, etc.}}
**V** ἡ ἀ. σου as a title, Your **worship,** {{Refs|PLips. 13 ii 20, etc.}}
**VI** an **engine of war,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus 38.11.}}
**VII** a **plaster,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Andromachus Poeta Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.531.}}

704:
** ἀρήν**
(_nominative_ only in Inscrr., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 1.4, 11.154 A 11 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), “SIG” 1027.9 (from Cos), ϝαρήν “GDI” 4964.2 (from Gortyn))}}; also ἄρης {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PGurob.” 22.40,42 (3rd c.BC), and ἄρνον [prev. work]3, +others}}; _accusative_ ἄρνα, _genitive_ ἀρνός, _dative_ ἀρνί, _dual_. ἄρνε: _plural_ ἄρνες, ἄρνας, ἀρνῶν, _dative_ ἀρνάσι{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.8.10, _Epic dialect_ ἄρνεσσι}}: —**lamb** (under a year old, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus 515, Istrosap. 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1672.12); ἀ. πρωτογόνων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.102, etc.; γαλαθηνῶν 5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 1.}}
**II** **sheep** of either sex, ἄρν᾽ ἕτερον λευκόν, ἑτέρην δὲ μέλαιναν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.103; ἄρνες κεραοί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.85.}}
**III** in _plural_, **stunted ears** of wheat, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.7.5}}. (Sanskrit _úrā_ 'sheep', **úraṇas** 'ram', 'lamb', **urabhras** 'ram', Armenian _ga[rdot]n_ 'lamb'; compare -ϝρην in πολύρρην.)

705:
** ἀριθμ-έω** [ᾰ],
_imperfect_ ἠρίθμεον as _trisyllable_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.204}}, _3rd.pers.singular_ ἠρίθμει{{Refs|13.218}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἠριθμησάμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 270d}}:—_passive_, _future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense ἀριθμήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1318}}; _future_ ἀριθμηθήσομαι{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.3Ki.3.8, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.68: _Epic dialect_}}_aorist_ _infinitive_ ἀριθμηθήμεναι (for -ῆναι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.124}}:—**number, count, reckon up,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.411, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.46, etc.}}; αὐτὰρ ἐγὼ δίχα πάντας.. ἑταίρους ἠρίθμεον **counted** them so as to halve them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.204}}; ἀριθμήσαντες **after numbering** the army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.60}}; οὐδεὶς πώποτ᾽.. ἠρίθμησε **stopped to count** the enemy, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 570: poetry, ἀριθμήσεις γαῖαν ἀπειρεσίην, ={μετρήσεις}, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.349 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus)}}]:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.111, 9.32:}} —_middle_, ἠριθμοῦντο **counted each for himself,** πλίνθους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.20.}}
**2** **count out, pay,** χρυσίον, ἀργύριον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.44, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.30, “IG” 5(1).1390.51 (from Andania), “Mon.Anc.Gr.” 7.22.}}
**3** **reckon, account,** ἐν εὐεργεσίας μέρει{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.166; ἀ. τινὰ κλυτόπαιδα “Anthologia Graeca” 9.262 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus); κέρδος τι ἀ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.158}}:—_passive_, **to be reckoned,** ἐν τοῖσι γενναίοισιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 729; ἐν γράμμασι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Jud.Voc.” 2; εἴς τινας 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.1.1}}; ἀριθμεῖσθαι τῶν φιλτάτων **as one of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1318; μακάρων 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.72. [-ῐθμ- 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 333, “Comica Adespota” 21.28 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator]}}

706:
** ἀριθμός** [ᾰ],
(ἁρ-{{Refs|“IG” 1.164)}}, ὁ, **number,** first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, λέκτο δ᾽ ἀριθμόν 4.451; ἀριθμῷ παῦρα 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 3; ἓν ἀριθμῷ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.6; ἀριθμὸν ἕξ [prev. author] 1.14, compare 50; ἐς τὸν ἀ. τρισχίλια [prev. author] 7.97}}; πλῆθος ἐς ἀ. the amount in point of **number,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 60; τὸν ἀ. δώδεκα 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 11.11; δύο τινὲς ἢ τρεῖς.. εἰς τὸν ἀ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 165; ἔλαττον μήτε ὄγκῳ μήτε ἀριθμῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 155a; οὔτ᾽ ἀριθμοῖς οὔτε μεγέθεσιν ἐλάττους [prev. author] “Lg.” 861e; σταθμῷ καὶ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.45; δι᾽ ἀ. καὶ μέτρου 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 16, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 620: proverbial, λέγειν ποντιᾶν ψάφων ἀριθμόν}} 'count the pebbles on the shore', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.46, compare 2.98}}; οὐ γιγνώσκων ψήφων ἀριθμούς, of a blockhead, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 19; οὔτ᾽ ἀριθμὸν οὔτ᾽ ἔλεγχον.. ἔχων 4th c.BC: Dionysius Comicus 3.13.}}
**2** **amount, sum,** πολὺς ἀ. χρόνου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.78; ἀ. τῆς ὁδοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.6}}; ἀ. [χρυσίου] a **sum** of money, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.2.16.}}
**3** ἀριθμῷ, _absolutely_, **in certain numbers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.58; but δένδρα ἀριθμῷ ὑμέτερα}}**by tale,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.72; ἀ. διδόναι 4th c.BC: Dionysius Comicus 3.6.}}
**4** **item** or **term** in a series, ὁ δεύτερος ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1014; τρίτον ὠδίνων ἀ. “epigram Gr.” 574; ναῦς πολλοὺς ἀ. ἄγνυται ναυαγίων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 410, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1461b24}}; τοὺς ἀ. τοῦ σώματος **points** of the body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 668d; τοὺς ἀ. ἑκάστου τῶν νοσημάτων 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 3; τὸ καλὸν ἐκ πολλῶν ἀ. ἐπιτελεῖσθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.45c}}: hence as a mark of **completeness,** πάντας τοὺς ἀ. περιλαβών{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.16}}; τοῦ καθήκοντος τοὺς ἀριθμούς the **sum total** of duty, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 3.1.}}
**5** **number, account,** as a mark of station, worth, rank, μετ᾽ ἀνδρῶν ἵζει ἀριθμῷ takes his place **among** men, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.449; εἰς ἀνδρῶν μὲν οὐ τελοῦσιν ἀ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 492; εἰς ἀ. τῶν κακῶν πεφύκαμεν [prev. author] “Hec.” 1186}}; ξενίας ἀριθμῷ πρῶτ᾽ ἔχειν ἐμῶν φίλων **in regard of** friendship, {{Refs|[prev. work] 794}}; δειλοὶ γὰρ ἄνδρες οὐκ ἔχουσιν ἐν μάχῃ ἀριθμόν have no **account** made of them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 519}}; οὐδ᾽ εἰς ἀ. ἥκει λόγων she comes not into my **account,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 1054; ἀ. οὐδεὶς οὐδὲ λόγος ἐστί τινος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.682f, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 27.6, Oracle texts cited in Scholia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.48.}}
**6** **mere number, quantity,** opposed to **quality,** ταῦτ᾽ οὐκ ἀ. ἐστιν, ὦ πάτερ, λόγων **a mere set** of words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 382}}; of men, οὐκ ἀ. ἄλλως not **a mere lot,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 476; ἀριθμός, πρόβατ᾽ ἄλλως 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1203}}; sometimes even of a single man, οὐκ ἀριθμὸν ἀλλ᾽ ἐτητύμως ἄνδρ᾽ ὄντα not **a mere unit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 997}}; also ἀριθμὸν πληροῦν to be **a mere cipher,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Choricius Rhetor “Milt.” 66.}}
**II** **numbering, counting,** μάσσων ἀριθμοῦ past **counting,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 2.23}}; especially in phrases, ἀ. ποιεῖσθαι τῶν νεῶν to hold **a muster** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.7; ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.7, etc.; παρεῖναι εἰς τὸν ἀ. [prev. work]II; εἴ τι δυνατὸν ἐς ἀ. ἐλθεῖν}} can be stated **in numbers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.72.}}
**III** **the science of numbers, arithmetic,** ἀριθμόν, ἔξοχον σοφισμάτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 459; ἀριθμῶν καὶ μέτρων εὑρήματα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 432; ἀ. καὶ λογισμὸν εὑρεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 274c, compare “R.” 522c: proverbial, εἴπερ γὰρ ἀριθμὸν οἶδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 360.19.}}
**IV** in Philos., **abstract number,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 4b23, “Metaphorical” 990a19, +others; ἀ. μαθηματικός [prev. work] 1090b35}}; ἀ. οὐσιώδης, opposed to τοῦ ποσοῦ, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.5.4}}; ἀ. ἑνιαῖος, οὐσιώδης, ἑτεροῖος, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 228.}}
**V** Grammars, **number,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Stoic. 3.214, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 634.16, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 32.2, +others}}; compare ἑνικός, δυικός, πληθυντικός.
**VI** **numeral,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 36.6}}, etc.; ὁ τέσσαρα ἀ.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.96}}; παιδὸς ἀ., ={δεκάτη}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1132.}}
**VII** **unknown quantity (x),** defined as πλῆθος μονάδων ἀορίστων, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus “Def.” 2.}}
**VIII** Rhet., **rhythm** in Prose, in _plural_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 23, “Dem.” 52, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1408b29; but also ἀριθμοὶ τῶν ἀρχαίων ποιητᾶν “SIG” 703.7 (Delph.).}}
**IX** **line** of a book, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Cit. 2.}}
**X** **sum of numerical values** of letters in a name, {{NT|NT.Rev.13.17, +others}}; φιλῶ ἧς ἀριθμὸς φμέ Pompeian Inscription in {{Refs|“Rend.Linc.” 10(1901).257.}}
**XI** **unit** of troops, = Latin _numerus_, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 5187 (6th c.AD), “BGU” 673 (6th c.AD), etc.}}; = **legio,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator}}**ad Ath.**280d, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus 5.26, “PLond.” 5.1711.69 (6th c.AD).}}
**XII** Astrology texts, mostly in _plural_, **degrees traversed in a given time,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 112, 2nd c.AD: Dorotheus Astrologus in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 6.107.30}}; τοῖς ἰδίοις ἀ. at her normal **speed,** of the moon, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.531}}; also of **degrees** of latitude, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 2.8, 3.1.}}
**XIII** in Medicine texts, in _plural_, **precise conditions,** παρόντων τῶν πρὸς τὴν φλεβοτομίαν ἀριθμῶν Herod._middle_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Rh.Mus.” 58.71, compare 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.3, probably in 1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.2; compare above 1.4. [ῐ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1132, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1203.] (ἀρῐ-θμός from root ἀρι-, compare ἐπάριτος (which see), νήριτος.)}}

709:
** ἀριστάω**,
_infinitive_ ἀριστᾶν, _Ionic dialect_ **-ῆν**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 3.68}}: _perfect_ ἠρίστηκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.39, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 212.25}}; of this tense the Comedy texts also used I _plural_ ἠρίσταμεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 496, 5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 22}}, _infinitive_ ἠριστάναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 60:}} — _passive_, _perfect_ ἠρίστημαι, see below [ᾱρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 815, etc.}}; ᾰρ only late, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.387 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus)}}]:—**take the** ἄριστον or **midday meal,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 416, “Eq.” 815}}; ἠρίστων, opposed to ἐδείπνουν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.12, compare “An.” 4.6.21}}: with _accusative_ _of things_, **breakfast on,** ἴα καὶ ῥόδα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 2.37, compare 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 122.5}}: _perfect_ _passive_ _impersonal_, ἠρίστηται δ᾽ ἐξαρκούντως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 377.}}
**2** **eat a second meal,** opposed to μονοσιτέω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “V M” 10, “Acut.” 30.}}

710:
** ἀριστερός** [ᾰ], ά, όν,
**left, on the left,** ἐπ᾽ ἀριστερά towards, i.e. on, **the left,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.526, +others; ἐπ᾽ ἀριστερὰ χειρός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.277; ἐπ᾽ ἀ. χειρῶν 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1266; ἐξἀριστερῶν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.4.1; ἐν τοῖσι ἀριστεροῖσι [prev. passage]}}
**2** ἀριστερά (with or without χείρ), ἡ, **left hand,** ἐξ ἀριστερῆς χειρός on **the left hand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.30; simply ἀριστερῆς χ. [prev. author] 4.34; ἐξ ἀριστερᾶς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 72c, etc.; οὑξ ἀριστερᾶς.. ναός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 7}}; ἐς ἀριστερὴν χεῖρα ἤιε, ἐν ἀριστερῇ ἔχειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.42.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **boding ill, ominous,** because to a Greek, looking northward, unlucky signs came from the left, ἀ. ἤλυθεν ὄρνις{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.242.}}
**4** **awkward, erring,** φρενόθεν ἐπ᾽ ἀριστερὰ ἔβας turnedst to **the leftward** of thy mind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 182 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀριστερὰ εἴληφας τὸ πρᾶγμα in a **sinister** sense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Comica Adespota” 22.67 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; τῷ ἀριστερῷ δέχεσθαι [λόγους] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.378b}}. (Prop. 'better', compare ἄριστο; euphemism (compare εὐώνυμος) to avoid ill-luck.)

714:
** ἀρκέω**,
_imperfect_ _3rd.pers.singular_ ἤρκει{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.440, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 278:}}_future_ ἀρκέσω: _aorist_ ἤρκεσα, _Doric dialect_ ἄρκεσα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.3:}} —_middle_, _aorist_ ἠρκεσάμην, _2nd pers.singular_ ἠρκέσω uncertain in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 213 (assuming variant)}}:—_passive_, _infinitive_ ἀρκέεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.33, ἀρκεῖσθαι poetical cited in Greg.Cor. p.425 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}: _perfect_ ἤρκεσμαι{{Refs 5th c.AD+|Sthenidas Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.63}}: _aorist_ ἠρκέσθην{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 35, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 83}}: _future_ ἀρκεσθήσομαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.94, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.8, etc.}}:—**ward off, keep off,** with _dative_ _person_ et _accusative_ _of things_, σάκος τό οἱ ἤρκεσε λυγρὸν ὄλεθρον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.289, compare 6.16; πατρίδι δουλοσύνην 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 101; κῆρας μελάθροις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1300 (Lyric poetry); ὅς οἱ ἀπὸ χροὸς ἤρκει ὄλεθρον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.440, compare 15.534; τοῦτό γ᾽ ἀρκέσαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 535}}; ὡς οὐκ ἀρκέσοι τὸ μὴ οὐ.. θανεῖν **would** not **keep off** death, {{Refs|[prev. work] 727.}}
**2** with _dative_ only, **defend,** πυκινὸς δέ οἱ ἤρκεσε θώρηξ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.529; οὐδ᾽ ἤρκεσε θώρηξ, without _dative_, 13.371.}}
**3** **assist, succour,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.131, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.261, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 824, “El.” 322, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1164.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _cognate_, **make good, achieve,** οὐδ᾽ ἔργα μείω χειρὸς ἀρκέσας ἐμῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 439.}}
**III** mostly in Trag., and always in Prose, **to be strong enough, suffice,** with _infinitive_, first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.3}}; ἀρκῶ σοι σαφηνίσαι (-σας Linwood) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 621 codices, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1209 (Lyric poetry): with}}_participle_, ἀρκέσω θνῄσκουσ᾽ ἐγώ my death **will suffice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 547; compare ἀρκοῦμεν ἡμεῖς οἱ προθνῄσκοντές σεθεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 383}}; ἔνδον ἀρκείτω μένων **let him be content** to stay within, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 76; ἀρκεῖν γὰρ οἶμαι μίαν ψυχὴν τάδ᾽ ἐκτίνουσαν [prev. author] “OC” 498; οὔτε ἰατροὶ ἤρκουν θεραπεύοντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.47}}; elliptically, σοφοὺς ὥσπερ σύ, μηδὲν μᾶλλον· ἀρκέσουσι γάρ [σοφοὶ ὄντες] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 576; ἀ. εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.5; πῶς ἡ πόλις ἀρκέσει ἐπὶ τοιαύτην παρασκευή; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369d}}; ταὐτὸν ἀρκεῖ σκῶμμα ἐπὶ πάντας **holds** equally for all, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 174a}}; ὅτ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἀρκεῖ [ἡ μάθησις] when it **avails** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 711.}}
**III.2** with _dative_, **suffice for, satisfy,** οὐδὲ ταῦτά τοι μοῦνα ἤρκεσε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.115, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 308, etc.}}
**III.3** **to be a match for,** with _dative_, ψιλὸς ἀρκέσαιμι σοί γ᾽ ὡπλισμένῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1123; πρὸς τοὺς πολεμίους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.84.}}
**III.4** _absolutely_, **to be enough, avail, endure,** ἀρκείτω βίος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1314; οὐδὲν γὰρ ἤρκει τόξα [prev. author] “Pers.” 278}}; **holdout, last,** ἐπὶ πλεῖστον ἀρκεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.31}}; οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἀρκῶ I **can hold out** no longer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 186 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὥστε ἀρκεῖν πλοῖα **to be sufficient** in number, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.13}}: frequently in _participle_, ἀρκῶν, οῦσα, οῦν, **sufficient, enough,** βίος ἀρκέων ὑπῆν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31, compare 7.28; τἀρκοῦντα}}**a sufficiency,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 865; ἀρκοῦσα ἀπολογία 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.10}}; ἀρκοῦντα or τἀρκοῦντα ἔχειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.1, “Smp.” 4.35; τῶν ἀρκούντων περιττὰ κτᾶσθαι [prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.2.21.}}
**III.5** _impersonal_, ἀρκεῖ μοι **'tis enough** for me, I **am well content,** with _infinitive_, οὐκ ἀρκέσει ποθ᾽ ὑμὶν.. εἴκειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1242, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.13}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἐμοὶ μὲν ἀρκεῖ τοῦτον ἐν δόμοις μένειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 80}}; ἀρκεῖ ἢν.., ὅτι.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.14, “Mem.” 4.4.9}}; ἔμ᾽ ἀρκεῖ βουλεύειν **'tis enough** that I.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 248}}; οὐκ ἀρκοῦν μοί ἐστι, with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.2}}; ἀρκεῖν δοκεῖ μοι it seems **enough,** seems **good,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1364.}}
**IV** in _passive_, **to be satisfied with,** with _dative_ _of things_, poetical cited in Greg.Cor.[same place]; ἔφη οὐκέτι ἀρκέεσθαι τούτοισι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.33, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 369e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107b15, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.329 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus), 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.5.3: _absolutely_, [prev. work] 3.6, etc.}}
**IV.2** later, with _infinitive_, **to be contented to** do, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.20.1, Ps.- 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philopatr.” 29, etc.}}

715:
** ἄρκτος**, ἡ,
**bear,** especially **Ursus arctos, brown bear,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.611, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 223, “hymnus ad Venerem” 159, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.191, etc.}}: the instances of the _masculine_ are uncertain {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Coloribus” 798a26 is inconclusive)}}, the _feminine_ being used even when both sexes are included, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 539b33.}}
**2** Ἄρκτος, ἡ, the constellation **Ursa Major,** Ἄρκτον θ᾽, ἣν καὶ Ἄμαξαν ἐπίκλησιν καλέουσιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.487, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.273, compare 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 120, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1154, etc.; τὰ ὑπὸ τὴν Ἄ. ἀοίκητα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.10; Ἄρκτου στροφάδες κέλευθοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 131 (Lyric poetry); Ἄρκτου στροφάς τε καὶ Κυνὸς ψυχρὰν δύσιν [prev. author] “fragment” 432.11}}: in _plural_, **the Greater and Lesser Bears,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 27}}; Ἄ. μικρά, μεγάλη, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.5.35, 36, compare 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 2.41.105.}}
**3** **the north,** πρὸς ἄρκτον τετραμμένος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.148, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 733 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.165, etc.; ἀπὸ ἄ. “IG” 5(2).444.11 (from Megalopolis), +others: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 5 and 19, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 118b, etc.}}
**3.b** ἡ ἑτέρα ἄ. **the south pole,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 362a32.}}
**II** ἄρκτος, ἡ, at Athens **a girl appointed to the service of Artemis Brauronia** or Ἀρχηγέτις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hyps.fragment” 57, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 645.}}
**III** a kind of **crab,** probably **Scyllarus arctus,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 549b23, compare 4th c.BC: Speusippus Philosophus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.105b, 4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 4.45, 4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 56.}}
**IV** ἄρκτου δένδρον, ={ἀκτῆ}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.173}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _ṛk[snull]as,_ Latin _ursus,_ etc.)

716:
** ἅρμα**, ατος, τό,
**chariot,** especially **war-chariot,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.231, etc.; frequently in _plural_ for _sing._, ἑσταότ᾽ ἐν θ᾽ ἵπποισι καὶ ἅρμασι 4.366, etc.; τὰ Λυδῶν ἄρματα 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.19; ἵππους ὑφ᾽ ἅρμασι ζευγνύναι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 190, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 111; ἵππους ὑφ᾽ ἅρματα ἄγειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 465; πῶλον.. ζυγέντ᾽ ἐν ἅρμασιν [prev. author] “Ch.” 795 (Lyric poetry)}}; opposed to ἁρμάμαξα (which see); also, **racing-chariot** drawn by horses, opposed to ὄχημα (a mule-car), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 106.5; ἅ. τέλειον “IG” 2.967.45; ἁρμάτων ὀχήματα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 662, compare “Ph.” 1190}}; **travelling-chariot,** {{NT|NT.Acts.8.28.}}
**2** **chariot and horses, yoked chariot,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.384, etc.; ἅ. τέθριππον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.14}}; ἅ. τετράορον, τέτρωρον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “P.” 10.65, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 483: _metaphorically_, τρίπωλον ἅ. δαιμόνων}} of three goddesses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 277 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** **team, chariot-horses,** ἅρμασιν ἐνδίδωσι κέντρον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HF” 881 (Lyric poetry); ἅρματα.. φυσῶντα καὶ πνέοντα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 902}}; ἅρματα τρέφειν keep **chariot-horses for racing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 11.5}}; ἅρματος τροφεύς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 834b.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, ἅ. θαλάσσης **a ship,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 4.230, +others, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.190.}}
**II** **a mountain district in Attica,** where omens from lightning were watched for: hence proverbial, ὁπόταν δἰ Ἅρματος ἀστράψη, i.e. **seldom** or **never,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.2.11}}; δι᾽ Ἅρματος alone, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.679c.}}
**III** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **unity,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 6.}}

718:
** ἁρμόζω**,
_Attic dialect_ ἁρμόττω, _Doric dialect_ ἁρμόσδω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.53}} (ἐφ-); _participle_ ἁρμόσσον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 37:}}_imperfect_ ἥρμοζον, _Doric dialect_ ἅρμ-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.11}}: _future_ ἁρμόσω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1318 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 31, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 263}}: _aorist_ ἥρμοσα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.333, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ ἅρμοξα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.12}} (συν-): _perfect_ ἥρμοκα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1459b32}}:— _middle_, _Epic dialect_ _imperative_ ἁρμόζεο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.162}} **-όζου** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 187}}: _future_ **-όσομαι**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.971}}: _aorist_ ἡρμοσάμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.32, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ ἁρμοξάμην{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 71}}: —_passive_, _perfect_ ἥρμοσμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 116 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 193d}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἅρμοσμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.124}}; _Doric dialect_ _infinitive_ ἁρμόχθαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.13.2}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers.singular_ ἅρμοκται{{Refs 5th c.AD+|Ecphantus Pythagoreus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.64}}: _aorist_ ἡρμόσθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 93a}}, _Doric dialect_ ἁρμόχθην{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.85}}: _future_ ἁρμοσθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 908}}:—**fit together, join,** especially of **joiner's** work, ἥρμοσεν ἀλλήλοισιν (i.e. τὰ δοῦρα) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.247}} (also in _middle_, **put together,** ἁρμόζεο χαλκῷ εὐρεῖαν σχεδίην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 162; ναυπηγίαν ἁρμόζων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 460; ἁρμόζειν χαίταν στεφάνοισι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7 6). 39; ἀρβύλαισιν ἁ. πόδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1189}}; ἁ. πόδα ἐπὶ γαίας **plant** foot on ground, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Or.” 233; ἁ. ποδὸς ἴχνια 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 182}}; ἐν ἁσυχαία βάσει βάσιν ἅρμοσαι (_aorist_ _imperative_ _middle_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 198; στόμ᾽ ἅρμοσον}}**kiss,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 763}}; ἁ. ψαλίοις ἵππους **furnish** them **with.., ** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 27 (Lyric poetry).}}
**b** generally, **adapt, accommodate,** ἁ. δίκην εἰς ἕκαστον **award** each his just due, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 36.17}}; σφισὶν βίοτον ἁ. **accord** them life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.98}}; **apply** a remedy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 687}}; **make ready,** τοὐπτάνιον{{Refs|Hegesippus Epigrammaticus Comedy texts 1.19}}:—_middle_, **accommodate, suit oneself,** πρὸς τὴν παροῦσαν πάντοθ᾽ ἁρμόζου τύχην Philem.[same place]; πρός τινα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 30}}; ἁ. σύνεσιν **acquire** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “νόμος” 2.}}
**2** of marriage, **betroth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.108; ἁ. κόρᾳ ἄνδρα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.117}}; ἁ. γάμον, γάμους,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 13, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 411}}:—_middle_, **betroth to oneself, take to wife,** τὴν θυγατέρα τινός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.32, 47}} (but _middle_ = _active_, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.2Cor.11.2); ἁ. ὡς ἐὰν αἱρῆται γάμῳ “POxy.” 906.7 (2nd-3rd c.AD):—}}_passive_, ἁρμόσθαι θυγατέρα τινὸς γυναῖκα **have** her **betrothed** or **married to** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.137}}; ὡς ἐκείνῳ τῇδέ τ᾽ ἦν ἡρμοσμένα as **troth was plighted** between him and her, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 570.}}
**3** **bind fast,** ἁ. τινὰ ἐν ἄρκυσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 231.}}
**4** **set in order, regulate, govern,** στρατόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.11}}:—_passive_, [νόμοις] οὐκ ἄλλοισιν ἁρμοσθήσεται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 908}}; κονδύλοις ἡρμοττόμην I **was ruled** or **drilled** with cuffs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1236.}}
**4.b** in the Spartan Constitution, **act as harmost,** ἐν ταῖς πόλεσιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 14.2, etc.: with _accusative_, ἁρμοστὴν ὃς ἥρμοζε τὴν Ἀσίαν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 17.}}
**5** in Music, **tune** instruments, τὸ σύμφωνον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 56a, etc.}}:—_middle_, ἁρμόττεσθαι ἁρμονίαν{{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 591d}}; ἁ. λύραν **tune one's** lyre,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 349e; Δωριστὶ ἁ. λύραν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 989; αὐλόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Harm.” 1 (but μέλη ἔς τι ἁ.}} **adapt** them **to** a subject, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 184)}}:—_passive_, of the lyre, ἡρμόσθαι **to be tuned,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 144e, compare “Phd.” 85e; ἁρμονίαν καλλίστην ἡρμ. [prev. author] “La.” 188d}}; ὁμονοητικὴ καὶ ἡρμοσμένη ψυχή **at harmony with itself,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 554e.}}
**6** **compose,** ᾆσμα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 19.17.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **fit well,** of clothes or armour, ἥρμοσε δ᾽ αὐτῷ [θώρηξ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.333; Ἕκτορι δ᾽ ἥρμοσε τεύχε᾽ ἐπὶ χροΐ 17.210; ἐσθὰς ἁρμόζοισα γυίοις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.80}}; ἆρ᾽ ἁρμόσει μοι (i.e. τὰ ὑποδήματα); {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 263}}; τοῖς τρόποις ἁ. ὥσπερ περὶ πόδα **fit** like a shoe, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Comedy texts 129; θώραξ περὶ τὰ στέρνα ἁρμόζων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.16.}}
**II.b** in Mathematics texts, **coincide with,** with _dative_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 612.14}}; **correspond,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 1.4.}}
**II.2** **suit, be adapted for,** τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 902 (Lyric poetry), “El.” 1293, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.6}}; τόδ᾽ οὐκ ἐπ᾽ ἄλλον ἁρμόσει **shall** not **be adapted** to another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1318; κἂν ἐπὶ τῶν θηρίων ἁρμόσειε λόγος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1281b19}}; εἴς τι, πρός τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 289b, 286d; πρὸς τὰς συνουσίας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.34, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.24}}; of medicines, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.2, +others}}; of an argument, **apply,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 209a9, +others; τὸ τοῦ Ξενοφάνους ἁρμόττει}}**is applicable,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1377a19.}}
**II.3** _impersonal_, ἁρμόζει **it is fitting,** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, σιγᾶν ἂν ἁρμόζοι σε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 731}}: c._infinitive_ only, λόγους οὓς ἁρμόσει λέγειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.42; πάντα τὰ τοιαῦτα ἁρμόττει καλεῖν [prev. author] 21.166; οὔτε ἁ. μοι οἰκεῖν μετὰ τοιούτων [prev. author] 40.57; τὰ τοιαῦτα ῥηθῆναι μάλιστ᾽ ἂν ἁρμόσειεν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.72.}}
**II.4** _participle_ ἁρμόζων, ουσα, ον, **fitting, suitable,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.129}}; ἡ ἁρμόζουσα ἀπόφασις the **appropriate** verdict, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1}} **Praef.;** ἀλλήλοις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 188d, +others: with _genitive_, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.44.1; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.5, etc.}}
**II.5** **to be in tune,** λύραν ἐπίτειν᾽ ἕως ἂν ἁρμόσῃ{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Macho Comicus 2.9.}}

719:
** ἁρμός**, ὁ,
(ἀραρίσκω) **joint,** in masonry, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 1.322; in metal-work, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 77.39}}; ὁ προσιών, ἀπιὼν ἁ., front and back **faces** of blocks, {{Refs|“IG” 7.3073.106,112 (Lebad.)}}, compare 22.463.40: _plural_, **fastenings** of a door, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1315, “Hipp.” 809}}; ἁ. χώματος λιθοσπαδής a fissure in the tomb made by tearing away the stones **at their joining,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1216}}; **chink in the fitting** of a door, ἁρμῷ τὴν ὄψιν προσβαλεῖν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.7, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 3.}}
**2** **bolt, peg,** ἁ. ἐν ξύλῳ παγείς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 360.12.}}
**3** **shoulder-joint,** {{Refs|Hippiatr. 34.}}

720:
** ἀρνέομαι**,
_future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 268, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 365; also ἀρνηθήσομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 527}} (ἀπ-), {{NT|NT.Luke.12.9:}}_aorist_ _passive_ ἠρνήθην frequently in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.60, etc.}}: also _aorist_ _middle_ ἠρνησάμην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1}}; rare in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1026, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.69, 3.324}}: _perfect_ ἤρνημαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.24}}:—**deny, disown,** τεὸν ἔπος ἀρνήσασθαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.212, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.358, etc.; ἀ. ἀμφὶ βόεσσιν “hymnus ad Mercurium” 390; ἀ. ἃ εἶπον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 303: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.174}}; ἀρνούμενοι ἔπαινοι **negative** praises, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.58a.}}
**2** **refuse,** τόξον.. δόμεναί τε καὶ ἀρνήσασθαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.345, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 408, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1; ἀ. γάμον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.249}}; ἀ. χρείαν **decline, renounce** a duty or office, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282; διαθήκην [prev. author] 36.34; κληρονομίαν “PFlor.” 61.49 (1st c.AD)}}; ζωὰν ἀ., of a suicide, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.473 (3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristodicus Epigrammaticus); δυνάμει τὸν βίον ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 11.163}}; ἀ. ἀνθρώπους **cast aside** humanity, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 2.10.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **say No, decline,** ὁ δ᾽ ἠρνεῖτο στεναχίζων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.304; αὐτὰρ ὅ γ᾽ ἠρνεῖτο στερεῶς 23.42, etc.}}
**4** in expressing **denial,** with _infinitive_, either without μή, **deny that**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 611, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 966}}; or with μή, **say that.. not**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 572, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.7, etc.}}; οὐδ᾽ αὐτὸς ἀρνεῖται μὴ οὐ.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 50.22}}; also οὐκ ἂν ἀρνοίμην τὸ δρᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 118}}; ἀ. ὅτι οὐ.., ὡς οὐ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.17, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.1, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.54.}}
**5** in expressing **refusal,** with _infinitive_, ἀ. εἶναι χρηστούς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.13}}: _poetry_ also with _participle_, οὐ γὰρ εὐτυχῶν ἀρνήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1158, compare “Or.” 1582.}}

721:
** ἀρνίον**, τό,
_diminutive_ of ἀρήν, **a little lamb,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.21, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 150.4, “PStrassb.” 24.7 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **sheepskin, fleece,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 43.}}

723:
** ἄροτρον** [ᾰ], τό,
(ἀρόω) **plough,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.374; πηκτὸν ἄ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.353, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1201, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.224, etc.; ἰλλομένων ἀρότρων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 340; ἀρότρῳ ἀναρρηγνύντες αὔλακας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.14: _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 515, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus “fragment” 4.6.}}
**b** ἡ ἀπ᾽ ἀρότρου πληγή, in boxing, **right-handed** blow, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.10.2, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 20.}}
**2** in _plural_, _metaphorically_ of the **organs of generation,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 12.46, +others}}
**II** ἄροτρα· γῆ, χώρα, πλέθρα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

724:
** ἁρπαγ-ή**, ἡ,
**seizure, robbery, rape,** first in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.13}}; ὀφλὼν ἁρπαγῆς δίκην found guilty **of rape,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 534; αἰτέειν δίκας τῆς ἁ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2; ἁρπαγῇ χρησαμένους [prev. work] 5}}; ἁρπαγὴν ποιεῖσθαι, ποιεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.52, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.12; ἐφ᾽ ἁ. τραπέσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.104, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.25; τοῦ κρητῆρος ἡ ἁ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.48}}: _plural_, of a single act, συνεπρήξαντο τὰς Ἑλένης ἁ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.94, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 351 (Lyric poetry), “Supp.” 510}}; Καδμείων ἁ., of the Sphinx, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1021 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **thing seized, booty, prey,** τοῦ φθάσαντος ἁ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 752}}; ἁ. κυσί, θηρσί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 1019, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 896}}; ἁρπαγὴν ποιεῖσθαί τι to make **booty** of a thing. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.62.}}
**III** **greediness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.17.}}
**IV** ἐν ἁρπαγῇ σελήνης when the moon is **invisible,** {{Refs|PMag.Par. 1.750.}}

725:
** ἁρπαγ-μός**, ὁ,
**robbery, rape,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.12a; ἁ. ὁ γάμος ἔσται 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 122.1.}}
**2** concrete, **prize to be grasped,** {{NT|NT.Phil.2.6; compare ἅρπαγμα 2.}}

726:
** ἁρπάζω**,
_future_ **-άξω**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.310, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 89.2}} **-άσω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 4.17}}, (ἀν-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1303}}; in _Attic dialect_ more commonly ἁρπάσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1118, “Ec.” 866, “Av.” 1460, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.5}}, (ἀν-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.59}}; _contraction_ ἁρπῶμαι, ἁρπᾷ{{LXX|LXX.Lev.19.13, +others}}: _aorist_ ἥρπαξα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.444, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.67, “IG” 4.951.11 (Epid.)}}; Trag. and _Attic dialect_ ἥρπασα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1634, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.101 (also 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.528, 17.62, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.156)}}: _perfect_ ἥρπακα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 372, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 481a}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἡρπασάμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 22, etc. ὑφ-αρπάσαιο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 921)}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ ἥρπασμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.27, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1079}} (ἀν-): {{Refs|3}}_pluperfect_ ἥρπαστο{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “El.” 1041; later ἥρπαγμαι 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.18.7}}, _infinitive_ **-άχθαι**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.11:}}_aorist 1_ ἡρπάσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1 and}} 4, etc., **-χθην** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.90 (variant), 8.115, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.74}}; later, _aorist 2_ ἡρπάγην [ᾰ] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 505, etc.}}: _future_ ἁρπᾰγήσομαι{{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.1Thes.4.17, 1st c.AD Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.10.3}}; _participle_ ἁρπάμενος (as if from ἅρπημι) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.59 (1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus), 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 1.340, +others}}, (ὑφ-) {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.619 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}:—**snatch away, carry off,** ὅτε σε πρῶτον Λακεδαίμονος ἐξ ἐρατεινῆς ἔπλεον ἁρπάξας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.444}}; ὡς δ᾽ ὅτε τίς τε λέων.. ἀγέλης βοῦν ἁρπάσῃ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 17.62; τοὺς δ᾽ αἶψ᾽ ἁρπάξασα φέρεν πόντονδε θύελλα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.48, compare 5.416; κλέψαι τε χἁρπάσαι βίᾳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 644; ἁ. τοῦ βασιλέος τὴν θυγατέρα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2; ἁ. [χρυσὸν] ὑπὲκ τῶν γρυπῶν [prev. author] 3.116; ἁ. καὶ φέρειν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.17}}: _absolutely_, **to be a robber,** ὁτιὴ ’πιώρκεις ἡρπακώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 428, compare “Pl.” 372}}; ἁρπάζειν βλέπει looks **thievish,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 181:}} —_passive_ (or _middle_), ἐκ χερῶν ἁρπάζομαι I **have** her **torn** from my arms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 661.}}
**2** **seize hastily, snatch up,** λᾶαν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.445; δόρυ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 624; τὰ ὅπλα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.8}}; ἁ. τινὰ μέσον **seize** him by the waist, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.107}}; λίθος ἥτις τὸν σίδηρον ἁρπάζει, of the magnet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 243: with _genitive_ of the part seized, ἁ. τινὰ τένοντος ποδός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 400: with _genitive_ _partitive_, ἁ. τούτων ἐνέτραγον 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 16.7: _absolutely_, ἀπογεύονται ἁρπάζοντες}}**greedily,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 354b}}:—_middle_ in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sacr.” 3.}}
**3** **seize, overpower, overmaster,** γλῶσσαν ἁ. φόβος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 259}}; **seize, occupy** a post, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.11}}; ἁρπάσαι πεῖραν **seize** an opportunity of attacking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 2; ἁ. τὸν καιρόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 15}}; **snap up,** ὥσπερ εὕρημα{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.30.}}
**4** **seize, adopt** a legend, of an author, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.156.}}
**5** **grasp with the senses,** ὀσμαὶ -όμεναι ταῖς ὀσφρήσεσιν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.647e.}}
**6** **captivate, ravish,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Jdt.16.9, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 28.}}
**7** **draw up** by means of a vacuum, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 647.28.}}
**II** **plunder,** πόλεις, τὰ ἐκ τῶν οἰκιῶν, τὴν Ἑλλάδα, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.5, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.55, etc.}}

727:
** ἅρπαξ**, ᾰγος, ὁ, ἡ,
(ἁρπάζω) **robbing, rapacious,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 137, variant in “fragment” 628, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.1.6; λύκοι 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1309 (variant{Ἄτρακας})}}]: also with _substantive_ _neuter_, ἅρπαγι χείλει{{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.272 (from Bianor)}}: _superlative_ ἁρπαγίστατος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 57.}}
**II** mostly as _substantive_,
**II.1** ἅρπαξ, ἡ, **rapine,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 356.}}
**II.2** ἅρπαξ, ὁ, **robber, peculator,** τῶν δημοσίων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 351; ὁ μὲν κλέπτης ὁ δ᾽ ἅ. 5th c.BC: Myrtilus Comicus 4}}; πάντες εἰσὶν ἅρπαγες (i.e. οἱ Ὠρώπιοι) {{Refs|Xeno 1.}}
**II.3** species of **wolf,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.304.}}
**II.4** **grappling-iron,** used in sea-fights, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.118, 4th c.BC: Moschio Tragicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.208d}}; **flesh-hook,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.3.7.}}

728:
** ἀρρᾰβών**, ῶνος, ὁ,
**earnest-money, caution-money,** deposited by the purchaser and forfeited if the purchase is not completed, ἀ. δοῦναί τινος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.20, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1259a12, Stilpoap. 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.118, “BGU” 446.18 (2nd c.AD)}}: _plural_, **deposits** required from public contractors, {{Refs|“IPE” 12.32 B 34 (from Olbia).}}
**2** generally, **pledge, earnest,** τὴν τέχνην ἔχοντες ἀ. τοῦ ζῆν{{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 123.6; τοῦ δυστυχεῖν.. ἀ. ἔχειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 697, cf. LXX.Gen.38.17, 18, NT.Eph.1.14.}}
**3** **present, bribe,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 17}}. (Semitic, probably Phoenician, word, Hebrew **'ērābōn**: frequently written ἀραβών, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 67.14 (2nd c.BC), NT.Eph.[same place], etc.)}}
**II** ἀρραβών· πρόδομα, καὶ ἄγκιστρον, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

729:
** ἄρρᾰφος**, ον,
(ῥάπτω) **without seam,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.John.19.23, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.7.4.}}
**2** **without sutures,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 11.19.}}

730:
** ἄρσην**, ὁ, ἡ, ἄρσεν, τό,
_genitive_ ἄρσενος, _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, and Trag.: _Attic dialect_ ἄρρην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 2.678 B 55, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 191c, etc.}}: _Aeolic dialect_, Cretan dialect, Epid., and {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} ἔρσην, which see; ἀρσ- prevails in LXX and **NT,** ἀρρ- is more common in Papyrus (exc. Papyrus Mag.): _nominative_ ἄρσης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 5(1).364.10 (_Laconian dialect_), “POxy.” 465.147 (2nd c.AD)}}:—**male,** μήτε τις οὖν θήλεια θεὸς.. μήτε τις ἄρσην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.7; βοῦν.. ἄρσενα 7.315; ἄρσενες ἵπποι 23.377, etc.; ἄρσην σπορά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 503; νηδύς [prev. author] “Ba.” 527 (Lyric poetry) (of the birth of Bacchus)}}; γονή{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Genit.” 7: ἄρσην, ὁ, or ἄρσεν, τό}} **the male,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 861, “Supp.” 393 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 665c, “Smp.” 191c, etc.; Ἀπόλλωνι.. θῆλυ καὶ ἄρσεν.. προσέρδειν “IG” 12(8).358 (Thasos, 5th c.BC)}}; οἱ ἄρσενες **the male sex,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45; τὸ ἄρσεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 737.}}
**2** **masculine,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Supp.” 951; φρένες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1204}}: _metaphorically_, **mighty,** κτύπος ἄρσην πόντου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1455 (Lyric poetry); Ἀχέροντος ἄρσενας χοάς [prev. author] “fragment” 480.3; ἄρσην βοή 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 125 (Lyric poetry); ἄ. φθόγγοι 3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 2.12}}; of plants, **robust, coarse,** opposed to θῆλυς (**tender, delicate**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.9.3, compare 2.2.6, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.1, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1196.}}
**3** of gender of nouns, **masculine,** ὀνόματα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 682.}}
**4** of sex in plants, ἀπὸ τοῦ ἄρρενος τοῖς θήλεσι βοήθεια{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.8.4}}:—but also, **coarse, tough,** γογγυλίς {{Refs|[prev. work] 7.4.3, compare 3.9.3: _comparative_ form}}ἀρρέντερος (compare θηλύτερος), κὰ τὠρρέντερον γένος in the **male** line, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).262.21(Mantinea, 5th c.BC).}}
**5** _adverb_ ἀρρένως{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.44.71. (Occurs without ϝ- on Cretan dialect Inscrr. which preserve ϝ-}}; cf. Sanskrit _ṛ[snull]abhás_ 'bull', Avest. **aršan-** 'man'.)

731:
** ἄρρητος**, ον,
also η, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 201}}:— **unspoken,** ἔπος προέηκεν ὅ πέρ τ᾽ ἄρρητον ἄμεινον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.466; ἄνδρες.. ῥητοί τ᾽ ἄ. τε 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 4; ἔστω ἄ. τὰ εἰρημένα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 189b, etc., compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.217}}; οὐκ ἐπ᾽ ἀρρήτοις γε τοῖς ἐμοῖς λόγοις not **without** warning **spoken** by me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 556; ἄ. κἀτελῆ φυλάξομαι [prev. author] “El.” 1012}}. _adverb_ -τως, σιγᾶν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 44 codices (ἀρρήκτως Reiske, ἀρράτως Bernays).}}
**II** **that cannot be spoken** or **expressed,** ἀδιανόητον καὶ ἄ. καὶ ἄφθεγκτον καὶ ἄλογον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 238c}}: hence, **unspeakable, immense,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.4; ἐπιθυμία 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.50 W.; εὐχαριστία [prev. author] “Lib.” p.51 O.}}
**III** **not to be spoken**: hence,
**III.1** **not to be divulged,** ἱροργίαι, ἱρά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.83, 6.135; σέβας ἀρρήτων ἱερῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 302; ἄ. σφάγια 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 41; ἄ. ἀβακχεύτοισιν εἰδέναι [prev. author] “Ba.” 472}}; διδακτά τε ἄρρητά τ᾽, i.e.things profane and **sacred,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 301}}; ἄ. κόρη the maid **whom none may name** (i.e. Persephone), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 63, compare “Hel.” 1307}}; ἀρρήτων θέσμια, i.e. of Demeter and Persephone, {{Refs|“IG” 3.713.6.}}
**III.2** **unutterable, horrible,** δεῖπνα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 203 (Lyric poetry); λώβη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 200 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἄρρητ᾽ ἀρρήτων 'deeds without a name', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 465 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.3** **shameful to be spoken,** ῥητόν τ᾽ ἄ. τ᾽ ἔπος{{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 1001, compare “Aj.” 214 (Lyric poetry), 773}}; ῥητὰ καὶ ἄ. ὀνομάζων 'dicenda **tacenda** locutus', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.122; πάντας ἡμᾶς ῥητὰ καὶ ἄ. κακὰ ἐξεῖπον [prev. author] 21.79}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.187.}}
**IV** of numbers, ἄρρητα, τά, **irrationals, surds,** opposed to ῥητά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 303b, compare “R.” 546c.}}

732:
** ἄρρωστ-ος**, ον,
(ῥώννυμι) **weak, sickly,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 634b14, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.465c}}. _adverb_ -τως, ἔχειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.14, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.12; διακεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.20.}}
**2** in moral sense, **weak, feeble,** τὴν ψυχήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 30, compare “Oec.” 4.2 (_comparative_).}}
**3** ἀρρωστότερος ἐς τὴν μισθοδοσίαν **remiss** in payment, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.83. [ᾰρωστος “Anthologia Graeca” 11.206 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus).]}}

736:
** ἀρτέμ-ων**, ονος, ὁ,
(ἀρτάω) **foresail** of a ship, {{NT+6th c.AD+|NT.Act.27.40, uncertain meaning in 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “de Mensibus” 2.12.}}
**II** **principal pulley** in a system, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 10.2.9 (in Latin form, = Gk. ἐπάγων).}}

737:
** ἄρτι** [ῐ],
_adverb_ **just, exactly,** of coincidence of Time, **just now** (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus):}}
**1** mostly of the present, with _present_ tense, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 998, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.158, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 534}}; opposed to πάλαι, with _perfect_, τέθνηκεν ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1283; βεβᾶσιν ἄ. [prev. author] “El.” 1386}}; ἄ. ἥκεις ἢ πάλα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43a; more fully ἄ. νυνί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1008}}; ἄ... νῦν or νῦν.. ἄ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 291a, 291b}}; later, ={νῦν}, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.26, NT.Gal.1.9, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.6.1}}; ἄ. καὶ πρώην **to-day** and yesterday, i. e. **very lately,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brut.” I, etc.}}; ἕως ἄ. till **now,** {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Matt.11.12, “POxy.” 936.23 (3rd c.AD)}}: with _substantive_, ὁ ἄ. λόγος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 153e}}; ἡλικίαν.. τὴν ἄ. ἐκ παίδων{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.25; ἡ ἄ. ὥρα NT.1Cor.4.11, “PMag.Lond.” 1.121.373}}; ἄ. μὲν.. ἄ. δὲ.. **now.. now.., at one time.. at another**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 4.}}
**2** of the past, **just now,** with _imperfect_, ἄ. βλάστεσκε uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 546, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 677, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 454b}}: with _aorist_, λέξας ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1272; καθῃμάτωσεν ἄ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1160}}; opposed to νῦν, ὃ ἄ. ἐρρήθη.. νῦν δὲ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.130d}}, compare 127c; ἐν τῷ ἄ., opposed to ἐν τῷ νῦν καὶ ἐν τῷ ἔπειτα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Men.” 89c.}}
**3** in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 26.7 (assuming variant)}} and later also of the future, **just now, presently,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 1, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μιθριδάτειος” 69, 2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracula” 94.2; condemned by 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 12}}; also, **just at present,** πλεύσεις, ἄ. δὲ οὔ{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracula” 92.7: with _imperative_, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 20.277, etc.}} (Perh. _cognate_ with Sanskrit _ṛtám_ 'ordinance', **ṛtás** 'correct'.)

739:
** ἄρτιος**, α, ον
(ος, ον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.25)}}: (ἄρτι):— **complete, perfect of its kind, suitable, exactly fitled,** ἄ. ἀλλήλοισι σπόνδυλοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 45}}; ἄρτια βάζειν speak **to the purpose,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.92, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.240}}; ὅτι οἱ φρεσὶν ἄρτια ᾔδη thought things **in accordance with** him, was of **the same** mind **with** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.326, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.248; ἄρτια μήδεσθαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.94}}; **meet, right, proper,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.40, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 946}}; ἄ. εἴς τι **well-suited** for.., {{Refs|“IG” 14.889.7 (from Sinuessa)}}; ἀρτιωτάτην ἔχειν τάξιν **most perfect,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.21.3.}}
**2** **full-grown,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.5.5}}; **sound,** of body and mind, νόος, σώμασιν, {{NT+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 154, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.33, cf. NT.2Tim.3.17.}}
**3** **prepared, ready,** with _infinitive_, ἄρτιοι πείθεσθαι, ποιέειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.27, 48; καταβιῶναι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.9.}}
**II** of numbers, **perfect,** i.e. **even,** opposed to περιττός (**odd**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 170.7, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e, +others}}; ἄ. πόδες **even number of** feet, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 489b22}}; ἐν ἀρτίῃσι (i.e. ἡμέρῃσι) happening on **the even** days, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.18}}; ἄ. χώρα, of the **even** feet in iambic and trochaic verse, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 5.1, 3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus [prev. cited]}}
**II.2** **exact, precise,** ἐτῶν ἀριθμὸν ὀγδοήκοντ᾽ ἀρτίων{{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 222b (Milet.).}}
**III** _adverb_ ἀρτίως **just, newly,** ={ἄρτι}, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 251, frequently in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III.1** of present time, with _present_, {{Refs|“Aj.” 678, “OT” 78, +others}}: with _perfect_, {{Refs|“OC” 892, +others}}
**III.2** of the past, with _imperfect_, {{Refs|“Tr.” 664, 674, etc.}}: with _aorist_,{{Refs|[prev. work] 346, “OT” 243, etc.}}
**III.3** with an _adjective_, ἀ. νεοσφαγής{{Refs|“Aj.” 898.}}
**III.4** **closely fitting,** καθηλῶσαί τί τινι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 3.11.13.}}
**IV** _neuter_ _plural_ ἄρτια, ={ἀρτίως}{{Refs 1st c.BC+|2, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.234 (1st c.BC: Erycius Epigrammaticus).}}

740:
** ἄρτος**, ὁ,
**cake** or **loaf of wheat-bread,** mostly in _plural_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.120, +others}}; ἄρτος οὖλος a whole **loaf,** {{Refs|17.343}}; collectively, **bread,** δούλιον ἄρτον ἔδων{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus “Supp.” 2.6; ἄ. τρισκοπάνιστος Batrachomyomachia 35}}; opposed to μᾶζα (**porridge**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 37.}} —Freq. in all writers.
**II** ἄρτος· βόλος τις, καὶ ὁ Ἀθηναίων ξένος, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

741:
** ἀρτύω**
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.771}}, _imperfect_ ἤρτῠον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below)}}, the only tenses in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus:}}_future_ ἀρτύσω [ῡ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1122}}: _aorist_ ἤρτῡσα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.12, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 303:}}_perfect_ ἤρτῡκα (κατ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 473}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ ἤρτῡμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus, see below:}}_aorist_ ἠρτύθην [ῡ] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 4.2.4: in _Attic dialect_ this Verb is chiefly used in compounds with κατά and ἐξ: (compare ἀραρίσκω):}} —like ἀρτύνω, **arrange, prepare, make ready,** of things requiring skill or cunning, e.g. of a smith, τὰ δ᾽ ἤρτυε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.379; σοὶ δὲ.. δόλον ἤρτυε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.439; τῷδ᾽ ἤρτυεν.. ὄλεθρον 16.448, compare 20.242; γάμον.. ἀρτύει 4.771; ἤρτυσαν τὴν ἐπιβουλήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.12; φόνον τινί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.25.2.}}
**II** in culinary sense, **dress savoury** meat, **season,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1122, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 303; ἀ. τὰ ὄψα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1118a29}}:—_passive_, κίχλαι.. εἰς ἀνάβραστ᾽ ἠρτυμέναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 108.23; ὄψῳ πονηρῷ πολυτελῶς ἠρτυμένῳ 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 335; τὰ πρὸς ἡδονὴν ἠρτυμένα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 14; ἠρτυμένος οἶνος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 51.}}
**III** **administer** property, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 12.32, “IG” 5(2).3.27 (Tegea, 4th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 192.}}
**IV** **bequeath,** {{Refs|Tab.Heracl. 1.106.}}

744:
** ἀρχαῖος**, α, ον,
(ἀρχή I) **from the beginning** or **origin:**
**I** mostly of things, **ancient,** σκότος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 106; ἐσθής 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.88; δόμοις ἐπασσάλευσαν ἀρχαῖον γάνος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 579 codices; Ζηνὸς ἀρχαίοις νόμοις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1382}}; χερὸς σῆς πίστιν ἀρχαίαν faith **firm for ever,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 1632 codices}}
**I.2** **old-fashioned, antiquated,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 317 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 984, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.14}}; of literary style, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 244.}}
**I.2.b** **simple, silly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 915, +others, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 205; -ότερος εἶ τοῦ δέοντος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 295c, etc.}}
**I.3** **ancient, former,** τὸ ἀ. ῥέεθον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75; τοῦ ἀ. λόγου [prev. author] 7.160; οὐ γὰρ δὴ τό γ᾽ ἀ. δέμας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 110}}; οἱ ἀ., opposed to οἱ ὕστερον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.16; ἀ. φύσις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 281, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 53, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 193c, etc.; φύσις καὶ κατάστασις ἀ. 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 278; coupled with παλαιός, παλαιὸν δῶρον ἀρχαίου θηρός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 555, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.51, D. [prev. cited]}}
**I.4** **old, worn out,** ὑποδήματα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.14; πινάκια “BGU” 781i1 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** of persons, Θέμιν.. ἀρχαίαν ἄλοχον Διός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 6.5}}; ἀ. θεαί, of the Erinyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 728; Πέλοψ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1292}}; οἱ ἀ. **the Ancients,** name given by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}} to the pre-Socratics, {{Refs|“Metaphorical” 1069a25, “GC” 314a6}}; in Lit. Crit., **ancient, classical** writers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 15, 67; in 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus}}, the philosophers down to Aristotle, {{Refs|5.1.9}}; in **NT, the Fathers,** {{NT|NT.Matt.5.21, +others}}
**II.2** **ancient, old,** βαλὴν ἀ., of Darius, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 657 (Lyric poetry); λάτρις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 609; ἑταῖρος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.8.1}}; οἱ ἀ. κύριοι the **original** owners, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“BGU” 992 ii 6 (2nd c.BC)}}; τὰς ἀ. πόλεις (banished from) their **original** cities, {{Refs|Polystr. p.22}} W.; ἀ. μαθητής an **original** disciple, {{NT|NT.Act.21.16; ἀ. μύστης “Inscription Magn.” 215 b}} παιδαγωγὸς ἀ., i.e **of old, formerly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 287, compare 853.}}
**III** _neuter_ as _adverb_, τὸ ἀρχαῖον, _Ionic dialect_ _contraction_ τὠρχαῖον, **anciently,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.56, 173, +others}}, _Attic dialect_ τἀρχαῖον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 326; ἀπὸ τοῦ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.117; ἐξ ἀρχαίων 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.14.}}
**III.2** regular _adverb_ ἀρχαίως **in olden style,** καινὰ ἀ. λέγειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 267b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.8, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.48; ἀ. καὶ σεμνῶς 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.183.}}
**IV** irregular _comparative_ ἀρχαιέστερος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 45 (on ἀρχέστατος see entry .)}}; usual _comparative_ **-ότερος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 469}}: _superlative_ **-ότατος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.105, etc.}}
**V** as _substantive_, τὸ ἀρχαῖον, of money, **prime cost,** πλέον τοῦ ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 3.2}}; **principal,** mostly in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1156, etc.; τἀρχαῖα ἀποδιδόναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.26, etc.}}; τῶν ἀρχαίων ἀπέστησαν lost their **capital,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.15: opposed to ἔργον, [prev. author] 27.10}}; opposed to πρόσοδοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.38.}}
**V.2** ἀρχαίη, ἡ, ={ἀρχή}, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 475.1, etc.}}

746:
** ἀρχή**, ἡ,
(see. ἄρχω) **beginning, origin,** νείκεος ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.116; πήματος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.81; φόνου 21.4, etc.}}; opposed to τέλος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.51, etc.}}; opposed to τελευτή, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 607, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 715e, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 1.1; ἀ. γενέσθαι κακῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97; ἀ. ποιήσασθαί τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.128, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.37, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.120, etc.; ἀ. λαβεῖν τινός 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.11; τὰς ἀρχὰς εἰληφέναι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.28.3}}; ἀρχὴν ὑποθέσθαι lay **a foundation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.2, etc.; βαλέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 326e}} (and _passive_, ἀρχαὶ βέβληνται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.8); ἀρχὴν ἄρχεσθαί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 36e}}; **source** of action, [ὁ ἄνθρωπος] ἔχει ἀρχὴν ἐλευθέραν{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.3.4.}}
**b** with Preps. in adverbial usages, ἐξ ἀρχῆς from **the beginning,** from **the first,** from **of old,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.188, 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 10, etc.; οὑξ ἀ. φίλος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 385; ἡ ἐξ ἀ. ἔχθρα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.3; τὸ ἐξ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 12.6; but πλουτεῖν ἐξ ἀ. πάλιν}}**anew, afresh,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 221; λόγον πάλιν ὥσπερ ἐξ ἀ. κινεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450a}}; ὁ ἐξ ἀ. λόγος the **original** argument, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 177c, etc.}}; τὰ ἐξ ἀ. the **principal sum,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280a30:—also ἀπ᾽ ἀ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 425, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.104, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.25, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 344, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 206d}}; κατ᾽ ἀρχάς in **the beginning,** at **first,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.153, 7.5; αὐτίκα κατ᾽ ἀ. [prev. author] 8.94; τὸ κατ᾽ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 798a, +others}}
**c** _accusative_ ἀρχήν, _absolutely_, **to begin with, at first,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, 2.28, 8.132; τὴν ἀρχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.20: _plural_, τὰς ἀρχάς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.22.8}}: frequently followed by a _negative_, not **at all,** ἀρχὴν μηδὲ λαβών{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.39, compare 1.193, +others; ἀ. δὲ θηρᾶν οὐ πρέπει τἀμήχανα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 92; ἀ. κλύειν ἂν οὐκ.. ἐβουλόμην [prev. author] “Ph.” 1239, compare “El.” 439, 5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 3, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.73, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 478c; sometimes with _Article_, τοῦτο οὐκ ἐνδέκομαι τὴν ἀ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.25; τὴν ἀ. γὰρ ἐξῆν αὐτῷ μὴ γράφειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.93.}}
**2** **first principle, element,** first so used by Anaximander, _accusative_ to {{Refs 4th c.BC+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 150.23, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 983b11, etc.; Ἡράκλειτος τὴν ἀ. εἶναί φησι ψυχήν [prev. author] “de An.” 405a25}}; of ὕλη and θεός, opposed to στοιχεῖα, {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 1.3.25}}; **practical principle** of conduct, τῶν πράξεων τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ τὰς ὑποθέσεις{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.10}}; **principles** of knowledge, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 995b8, +others}}
**3** **end, corner,** of a bandage, rope, sheet, etc., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.60, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 9, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 762, 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 18.14, NT.Act.10.11}}; of a compound pulley, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 84.14.}}
**4** in Mathematics texts, **origin** of a curve, τῆς ἕλικος{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἑλίκων” 11 “Def.” 2, etc.; ξυνὸν ἀ. καὶ πέρας ἐπὶ κύκλου περιφερείας 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 103.}}
**5** **branch** of a river, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.2.10 (_plural_).}}
**6** **sum, total,**{{LXX|LXX.Num.1.2.}}
**7** **vital organs** of the body, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.318, +others}}
**II** **first place** or **power, sovereignty** (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), Διὸς ἀρχά 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.64, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6, etc.; γενέσθαι ἐπ᾽ ἀρχῆς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284b2: _metaphorically_, μεγάλην μεντἂν ἀ. εἴης εὑρηκώς}} of a stroke of fortune, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.196: _plural_, ἀρχαὶ πολισσονόμοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 864 (Lyric poetry); τὰς ἐμὰς ἀρχὰς σέβων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 744, etc.: with _genitive_ _of things_, τῆσδ᾽ ἔχων ἀρχὴν χθονός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 737}}; ἀ. τῶν νεῶν, τῆς θαλάσσης, **power over** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.90, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.7, etc.: proverbial, ἀ. ἄνδρα δείξει Biasap. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1130a1, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 48}}; **method of government,** οὐδὲ τὴν ἄλλην ἀ. ἐπαχθής{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54.}}
**II.2** **empire, realm,** Κύρου, Περδίκκου ἀ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.128, etc.}}
**II.3** **magistracy, office,** ἀρχὴν ἄρχειν, παραλαμβάνειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80, 4.147; καταστήσας τὰς ἀ. καὶ ἄρχοντας ἐπιστήσας [prev. author] 3.89}}; εἰς ἀ. καθίστασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.70}}; εἰς τὴν ἀ. εἰσιέναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.72, etc.}}; ἀ. λαχεῖν to obtain **an office,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 57.25; Ἑλληνοταμίαι τότε πρῶτον κατέστη ἀ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.96; ἐνιαύσιος ἀ. [prev. author] 6.54}}; ἀ. χειροτονητή, κληρωτή, Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.21; withsg. Noun, Κυθηροδίκης ἀ. ἐκ τῆς Σπάρτης διέβαινεν αὐτόσε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.53}}; **term of office,** ἀρχῆς λοιποὶ αὐτῷ δύο μῆνες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.42; ἀρχαὶ καὶ λειτουργίαι “POxy.” 119.16 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.4** in _plural_, αἱ ἀρχαί **the authorities, the magistrates,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.47, cf. Canon Laws texts cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.83}}; ἐν ταῖς ἀ. εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54}}; ἡ ἀρχή collectively, 'the board', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.22, compare “IG” 1.229, etc.; παραδιδόναι τινὰ τῇ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.48}}; but ἡ ἀ., of a single **magistrate,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PHal. 1.226 (3rd c.BC)}}; κατ᾽ ἀρχῆς γὰρ φιλαίτιος λεώς against **authority,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 485; πομποὺς ἀρχάς [prev. author] “Ag.” 124 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.5** **command,** i.e. **body of troops,** {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.13.17, +others}}
**II.6** _plural_, **heavenly powers,** {{NT+5th c.AD+|NT.Rom.8.38, +others, compare 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 96}}; **powers of evil,** {{NT|NT.Eph.6.12, +others}}
**III** ={εἶδος μελίσσης ἀκέντρου}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

747:
** ἀρχηγός**,
_Doric dialect_ ἀρχᾱγός, όν, **beginning, originating,** λόγος ἀρχηγὸς κακῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 881}}; **primary, leading, chief,** Τροίας ἀ. τιμάς{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 196 (Lyric poetry); δύο φλέβες ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 666b25.}}
**II** as _substantive_, **founder,** of a tutelary hero, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 60}}; as _feminine_, **ancestral heroine,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 8.51; τοῦ γένους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.28, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.56; τῆς πόλεως θεὸς ἀ. τίς ἐστιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 21e}}; **founder of a family,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1162a4.}}
**II.2** **prince, chief,** Δία ἀ. θεῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.179, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 259}}; **chief captain, leader,** Ἑλλάνων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 138; Βεβρύκων 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.110; ἀ. ἱερέων “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 6798 (from Dijon), compare 2882 (Milet.).}}
**II.3** **first cause, originator,** κοπίδων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 81; πράγματος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.4, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.101; συγχύσεως “SIG” 684.8 (Dyme, 2nd c.BC)}}; φόνου{{NT+4th c.BC+|“POxy.” 1241 iii 35; σωτηρίας NT.Heb.2.10; Θαλῆς ὁ τῆς τοιαύτης ἀ. φιλοσοφίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 983b20; τῆς τέχνης 3rd c.BC(?): Sosipater Comicus 1.14; τὸ ἀ.}}**the originating power,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 401d, compare “Sph.” 243d}}; **primary, fundamental,** ἀρχηγὸν ἡ φωνή{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.19.}}

752:
** ἀρχι-συνάγωγος** [ᾰ], ὁ,
**ruler of a synagogue,** {{NT|NT.Mark.5.22, +others, “IG” 14.2304}}, Ramsay **Cities and Bishoprics** No.559:—hence **ἀρχι-συνᾰγωγέω**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BCH” 8.463 (Thessalonica, 2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **master of a guild** or **company,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae ad res Romanas pertinentes 1.782 (from Thrace), etc.}}

753:
** ἀρχι-τέκτων**, ονος, ὁ,
**chief-artificer, master-builder, director of works,** τοῦ ὀρύγματος, τῆς γεφύρας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.60, 4.87}}; opposed to χειροτέχνης, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 981a30}}; opposed to ἐργατικός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 259e}}; **commissioner of works,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 2.403, +others, [prev. work]9(1).694.145 (from Corcyra), “SIG” 284.12 (Chios, from Erythrae), etc.; ἀ. τοῦ ναοῦ [prev. work]494.3 (Delph.); ἀ. ἐπὶ τὰ ἱερά “IG” 2.404.}}
**b** _plural_, **board of naval constructors,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 46.1.}}
**2** generally, **author, contriver,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 477; ἀ. κύριος τῆς ἡδονῆς 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 149.2; ἀ. τῆς ἐπιβουλῆς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.11; τοῦ τέλους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1152b2}}; τοὺς ταῖς διανοίαις ἀ. τινός **those that direct activities** by thought, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1325b23.}}
**II** at Athens, **manager of the state theatre and of the Dionysia,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.28, “IG” 2.335.}}

755:
** ἀρχι-τρίκλῑνος**, ὁ,
**president of a banquet** (**triclinium**), {{NT|NT.John.2.9.}}
**2** **head-waiter,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 7.27.}}

756:
** ἄρχω**,
_Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ ἀρχέμεναι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.154:}}_imperfect_ ἦρχον{{Refs|[prev. work] 2.378, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ ἆρχον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).51}}: _future_ ἄρξω{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.667, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 940, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.144:}}_aorist_ ἦρξα, _Epic dialect_ ἄρξα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.230, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἦρχα{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3487.14 (from Thyatira), Canon Laws texts cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.851f}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.90, etc.}}; non-thematic _participle_ ἄρχμενος{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.56, +others}}: _imperfect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.93, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.28:}}_future_ ἄρξομαι (in _middle_ sense, see below) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.97, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 442, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.2}}; _Doric dialect_ ἀρξεῦμαι{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.95}}: _aorist_ ἠρξάμην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.310, etc.:}} —_passive_, _perfect_ ἦργμαι only in _middle_ sense, see below {{Refs|1.2}}: _aorist_ ἤρχθην, ἀρχθῆναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1277b13, see below 11.4}}:—**to be first,**
**I** in Time, **begin, make a beginning,** _active_ and _middle_ (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus the _active_ is more frequently, in _Attic dialect_ Prose the _middle_, especially where personal action is emphasized), πολέμου ἄρχειν}}**to be the aggressor,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.53}}; π. ἄρχεσθαι **to begin one's** operations, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.6}}; ἄρχειν τοῦ λόγου **to open** a conversation, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.6.6}}; ἄρχεσθαι τοῦ λόγου **to begin one's** speech, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.2.7. Constr.:}}
**I.1** mostly with _genitive_, **make a beginning of,** ἄρχειν πολέμοιο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.335; μύθων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.68; τῶν ἀδικημάτων πρῶτον τοῦτο ἄρξαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2; ἦρξεν ἐμβολῆς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 409; τοῦ κακοῦ [prev. work] 353}}; ἄρχειν χειρῶν ἀδίκων, ἄρχειν τῆς πληγῆς, **strike the first** blow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.1 and 2:}} — in _middle_ in religious sense, = {ἀπάρχεσθαι}, ἀρχόμενος μελέων **beginning with** the limbs, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.428, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 651}}; but _active_, σπονδαῖσιν ἄρξαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).37.}}
**I.2** with _genitive_, **begin from** or **with**.., ἐν σοὶ μὲν λήξω σέο δ᾽ ἄρξομαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.97; ἄρχεσθαι Διός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.25}}; πόθεν ἄρξωμα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 855; πόθεν ποτὲ ἦρκται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 5}}; ἄρχεσθαι, ἦρχθαι ἔκ τινος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.199, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 11}}; ἀπό τινος frequently in Prose, ἀρξάμενοι αὐτίκα ἀπὸ παιδίων **even** from boyhood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.12}}; but more commonly ἐκ παίδων, ἐκ παιδός, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408d, “Thg.” 128d:}} —ἀπό in non-temporal relations, ἀρξάμενος ἀπὸ σοῦ. i.e. **including** yourself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 471c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.297; ἀπὸ τῶν πατέρων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.15; μέχρι τῶν δώδεκα ἀπὸ μιᾶς ἀρξάμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 771c}}; ἀφ᾽ ἱερῶν ἠργμένη ἀρχή{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 771a; ἀφ᾽ Ἑστίας ἀρχόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 846.}}
**I.3** with _genitive_ _of things_ et _dative_ _person_, ἄρχε θεοῖς δαιτός **begin** a banquet to the gods, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.95; τοῖς ἄρα μύθων ἦρχε 2.433, etc.; τῇσι δὲ.. ἄρχετο μολπῆς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.101; ἦρξε τῇ πόλει ἀνομίας τὸ νόσημα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53, compare 12; τὴν ἡμέραν ἄρχειν ἐλευθερίας τῇ Ἑλλάδι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.23; ἡμὶν οὐ σμικρῶν κακῶν ἦρξεν τὸ δῶρον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 871.}}
**I.4** with _accusative_, ἄρχειν ὁδόν τινι, **show** him the way, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.107 (but also ἄρχειν ὁδοῖο}} **lead** the way, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.237): _absolutely_ (i.e. ὁδόν), ἄρχε δ᾽ Ἀθήνη 3.12; σὺ μὲν ἄρχε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.69; ἦ ῥα καὶ ἄρχε λέχοσδε κιών 3.447; ἦρχε δ᾽ ἄρα σφιν Ἄρης 5.592, compare below 11.2}}: with other accusatives, ἄρχειν ὕμνον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.10; ἅπερ ἦρξεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1529 (Lyric poetry); λυπηρόν τι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 552; ὕβριν [prev. author] “fragment” 368.}}
**I.5** of actions, σέο δ᾽ ἕξεται ὅττι κεν ἄρχῃ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.102: frequently with}}_infinitive_, τοῖσιν δ᾽ ἦρχ᾽ ἀγορεύειν among them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.571, etc.; ἦρχε νέεσθαι, ἦρχ᾽ ἴμεν, 2.84, 13.329; ἄρχετε νῦν νέκυας φορέειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.437, etc.; ὑφαίνειν ἤρχετο μῆτιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.324; ἤρξαντο οἰκοδομεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.107; ἡ νόσος ἤρξατο γενέσθαι [prev. author] 2.47}}: with _participle_, of continued action or condition, ἦρχον χαλεπαίνων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.378; ἢν ἄρξῃ ἀδικέων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.119; ἡ ψυχὴ ἄρχεται ἀπολείπουσα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.26; πόθεν ἂν ὀρθῶς ἀρξαίμεθα ἐπαινοῦντε; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 237a, compare “Tht.” 187a (but ἄ. ἐπαινεῖν [prev. author] “Phdr.” 241e); ἄρξομαι διδάσκων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.2 (but ἤρξω μανθάνειν [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.5.22).}}
**I.6** _absolutely_, ἄρχε **take the lead!** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.69}}: generally, **begin,** ἄρχειν [τὴν ἐκεχειρίαν] τήνδε τὴν ἡμέραν Indut. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118, cf. Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.42}}; τὸ ἄρχον, opposed to τὸ ἑπόμενον, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 234}}: _participle_ ἀρχόμενος **at first,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.3, “Cyn.” 3.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.54}}; **at the beginning,** ἀρχομένου δὲ πίθου καὶ λήγοντος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 368, compare “fragment” 192.4; ἀρχομένοισιν ἢ καταπαυομένοισι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1246; ἄρχεται ὁ πόλεμος ἐνθένδε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.1; ἅμα ἦρι ἀρχομένῳ [prev. passage]; θέρους εὐθὺς ἀρχομένου [prev. work] 47.}}
**I.7** Grammars, of a word, ἄ. ἀπὸ φωνήεντος{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 633.27}}; ἡ ἄρχουσα (i.e. συλλαβή) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.13.}}
**II** in point of Place or Station, **rule, govern, command,**
**II.1** mostly with _genitive_, **rule, be leader of**.., Βοιωτῶν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.494, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.1, etc.}}
**II.2** less frequently with _dative_, ἀνδράσιν ἦρξα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.230, compare 471, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.805, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.4, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 940, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 666, “IA” 337, “IG” 7.2830 (from Hyettus), etc.}}; also ἐν δ᾽ ἄρα τοῖσιν ἦρχ᾽ **held command** among them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.690, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238a: with}}_infinitive_ added, ἄρχε Μυρμιδόνεσσι μάχεσθαι **led** them **on** to fight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.65.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, **rule,** ὅσον τό τ᾽ ἄρχειν καὶ τὸ δουλεύειν δίχα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 927, compare “Pers.” 774}}; especially **hold a magistracy,** ὁκοῖόν τε εἴη ἄρχειν μετὰ τὸ βασιλεύειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.67}}; at Athens, etc., **to be archon,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.178}}; ἀρχάς, ἀρχὴν ἄρχειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54}}; ἄρχειν τὴν ἐπώνυμον (with or without ἀρχήν) {{Refs|“IG” 3.659, 693, “SIG” 872.7.}}
**II.4** _passive_, with _future_ ἄρξομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.159, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.45, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 589, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.7; but ἀρχθήσομαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1259b40, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 65.10}}:—**to be ruled, governed,** etc., ὑπό τινος{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.127; ἔκ τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 264, “Ant.” 63; ὑπό τινι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91, 103; σφόδρα ὑπό τινος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.92; ἄρχε πρῶτον μαθὼν ἄρχεσθαι 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.60, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326d; δύνασθαι καὶ ἄρχεσθαι καὶ ἄρχειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1277b14; οἱ ἀρχόμενοι}}**subjects,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2 6.19, etc.}}

757:
** ἄρχω**,
_Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ ἀρχέμεναι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.154:}}_imperfect_ ἦρχον{{Refs|[prev. work] 2.378, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ ἆρχον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).51}}: _future_ ἄρξω{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.667, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 940, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.144:}}_aorist_ ἦρξα, _Epic dialect_ ἄρξα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.230, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἦρχα{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3487.14 (from Thyatira), Canon Laws texts cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.851f}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.90, etc.}}; non-thematic _participle_ ἄρχμενος{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.56, +others}}: _imperfect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.93, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.28:}}_future_ ἄρξομαι (in _middle_ sense, see below) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.97, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 442, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.2}}; _Doric dialect_ ἀρξεῦμαι{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.95}}: _aorist_ ἠρξάμην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.310, etc.:}} —_passive_, _perfect_ ἦργμαι only in _middle_ sense, see below {{Refs|1.2}}: _aorist_ ἤρχθην, ἀρχθῆναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1277b13, see below 11.4}}:—**to be first,**
**I** in Time, **begin, make a beginning,** _active_ and _middle_ (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus the _active_ is more frequently, in _Attic dialect_ Prose the _middle_, especially where personal action is emphasized), πολέμου ἄρχειν}}**to be the aggressor,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.53}}; π. ἄρχεσθαι **to begin one's** operations, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.6}}; ἄρχειν τοῦ λόγου **to open** a conversation, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.6.6}}; ἄρχεσθαι τοῦ λόγου **to begin one's** speech, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.2.7. Constr.:}}
**I.1** mostly with _genitive_, **make a beginning of,** ἄρχειν πολέμοιο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.335; μύθων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.68; τῶν ἀδικημάτων πρῶτον τοῦτο ἄρξαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2; ἦρξεν ἐμβολῆς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 409; τοῦ κακοῦ [prev. work] 353}}; ἄρχειν χειρῶν ἀδίκων, ἄρχειν τῆς πληγῆς, **strike the first** blow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.1 and 2:}} — in _middle_ in religious sense, = {ἀπάρχεσθαι}, ἀρχόμενος μελέων **beginning with** the limbs, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.428, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 651}}; but _active_, σπονδαῖσιν ἄρξαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).37.}}
**I.2** with _genitive_, **begin from** or **with**.., ἐν σοὶ μὲν λήξω σέο δ᾽ ἄρξομαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.97; ἄρχεσθαι Διός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.25}}; πόθεν ἄρξωμα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 855; πόθεν ποτὲ ἦρκται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 5}}; ἄρχεσθαι, ἦρχθαι ἔκ τινος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.199, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 11}}; ἀπό τινος frequently in Prose, ἀρξάμενοι αὐτίκα ἀπὸ παιδίων **even** from boyhood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.12}}; but more commonly ἐκ παίδων, ἐκ παιδός, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408d, “Thg.” 128d:}} —ἀπό in non-temporal relations, ἀρξάμενος ἀπὸ σοῦ. i.e. **including** yourself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 471c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.297; ἀπὸ τῶν πατέρων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.15; μέχρι τῶν δώδεκα ἀπὸ μιᾶς ἀρξάμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 771c}}; ἀφ᾽ ἱερῶν ἠργμένη ἀρχή{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 771a; ἀφ᾽ Ἑστίας ἀρχόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 846.}}
**I.3** with _genitive_ _of things_ et _dative_ _person_, ἄρχε θεοῖς δαιτός **begin** a banquet to the gods, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.95; τοῖς ἄρα μύθων ἦρχε 2.433, etc.; τῇσι δὲ.. ἄρχετο μολπῆς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.101; ἦρξε τῇ πόλει ἀνομίας τὸ νόσημα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53, compare 12; τὴν ἡμέραν ἄρχειν ἐλευθερίας τῇ Ἑλλάδι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.23; ἡμὶν οὐ σμικρῶν κακῶν ἦρξεν τὸ δῶρον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 871.}}
**I.4** with _accusative_, ἄρχειν ὁδόν τινι, **show** him the way, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.107 (but also ἄρχειν ὁδοῖο}} **lead** the way, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.237): _absolutely_ (i.e. ὁδόν), ἄρχε δ᾽ Ἀθήνη 3.12; σὺ μὲν ἄρχε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.69; ἦ ῥα καὶ ἄρχε λέχοσδε κιών 3.447; ἦρχε δ᾽ ἄρα σφιν Ἄρης 5.592, compare below 11.2}}: with other accusatives, ἄρχειν ὕμνον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.10; ἅπερ ἦρξεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1529 (Lyric poetry); λυπηρόν τι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 552; ὕβριν [prev. author] “fragment” 368.}}
**I.5** of actions, σέο δ᾽ ἕξεται ὅττι κεν ἄρχῃ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.102: frequently with}}_infinitive_, τοῖσιν δ᾽ ἦρχ᾽ ἀγορεύειν among them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.571, etc.; ἦρχε νέεσθαι, ἦρχ᾽ ἴμεν, 2.84, 13.329; ἄρχετε νῦν νέκυας φορέειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.437, etc.; ὑφαίνειν ἤρχετο μῆτιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.324; ἤρξαντο οἰκοδομεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.107; ἡ νόσος ἤρξατο γενέσθαι [prev. author] 2.47}}: with _participle_, of continued action or condition, ἦρχον χαλεπαίνων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.378; ἢν ἄρξῃ ἀδικέων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.119; ἡ ψυχὴ ἄρχεται ἀπολείπουσα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.26; πόθεν ἂν ὀρθῶς ἀρξαίμεθα ἐπαινοῦντε; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 237a, compare “Tht.” 187a (but ἄ. ἐπαινεῖν [prev. author] “Phdr.” 241e); ἄρξομαι διδάσκων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.2 (but ἤρξω μανθάνειν [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.5.22).}}
**I.6** _absolutely_, ἄρχε **take the lead!** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.69}}: generally, **begin,** ἄρχειν [τὴν ἐκεχειρίαν] τήνδε τὴν ἡμέραν Indut. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118, cf. Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.42}}; τὸ ἄρχον, opposed to τὸ ἑπόμενον, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 234}}: _participle_ ἀρχόμενος **at first,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.3, “Cyn.” 3.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.54}}; **at the beginning,** ἀρχομένου δὲ πίθου καὶ λήγοντος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 368, compare “fragment” 192.4; ἀρχομένοισιν ἢ καταπαυομένοισι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1246; ἄρχεται ὁ πόλεμος ἐνθένδε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.1; ἅμα ἦρι ἀρχομένῳ [prev. passage]; θέρους εὐθὺς ἀρχομένου [prev. work] 47.}}
**I.7** Grammars, of a word, ἄ. ἀπὸ φωνήεντος{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 633.27}}; ἡ ἄρχουσα (i.e. συλλαβή) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.13.}}
**II** in point of Place or Station, **rule, govern, command,**
**II.1** mostly with _genitive_, **rule, be leader of**.., Βοιωτῶν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.494, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.1, etc.}}
**II.2** less frequently with _dative_, ἀνδράσιν ἦρξα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.230, compare 471, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.805, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.4, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 940, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 666, “IA” 337, “IG” 7.2830 (from Hyettus), etc.}}; also ἐν δ᾽ ἄρα τοῖσιν ἦρχ᾽ **held command** among them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.690, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238a: with}}_infinitive_ added, ἄρχε Μυρμιδόνεσσι μάχεσθαι **led** them **on** to fight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.65.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, **rule,** ὅσον τό τ᾽ ἄρχειν καὶ τὸ δουλεύειν δίχα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 927, compare “Pers.” 774}}; especially **hold a magistracy,** ὁκοῖόν τε εἴη ἄρχειν μετὰ τὸ βασιλεύειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.67}}; at Athens, etc., **to be archon,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.178}}; ἀρχάς, ἀρχὴν ἄρχειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54}}; ἄρχειν τὴν ἐπώνυμον (with or without ἀρχήν) {{Refs|“IG” 3.659, 693, “SIG” 872.7.}}
**II.4** _passive_, with _future_ ἄρξομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.159, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.45, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 589, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.7; but ἀρχθήσομαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1259b40, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 65.10}}:—**to be ruled, governed,** etc., ὑπό τινος{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.127; ἔκ τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 264, “Ant.” 63; ὑπό τινι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91, 103; σφόδρα ὑπό τινος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.92; ἄρχε πρῶτον μαθὼν ἄρχεσθαι 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.60, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326d; δύνασθαι καὶ ἄρχεσθαι καὶ ἄρχειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1277b14; οἱ ἀρχόμενοι}}**subjects,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2 6.19, etc.}}

758:
** ἄρχων**, οντος, ὁ,
(_participle_ of ἄρχω) **ruler, commander,** νεός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33: _absolutely_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 674, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 668, etc.}}; **chief, king,** Ἀσίας{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 73}}; ἄ. τοῦ κόσμου τούτου, of Satan, {{NT|NT.John.16.11, +others}}
**II** as official title, **chief magistrate,** especially at Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126, etc.; οἱ ἐννέα ἄρχοντες “IG” 2.163}}; οἱ ἄ., at Sparta, **the authorities,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.106: _sing._, ὁ ἄρχων}} the **eponymous magistrate of the year,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 1.52, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 3.3, etc.}}; so in Boeotia, at Delphi, Delos, and elsewhere, {{Refs|“IG” 7.2407, “SIG” 295.18, “IG” 2.814, etc.}}; = Latin _consul,_ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.39.1.}}
**II.2** **governor** of a dependency or province, e.g. in the Athenian Empire, {{Refs|“IG” 1.62b19, etc.}}; of a Roman **governor**, {{Refs|“OGI” 441.59}}; = **praefectus,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.26.5.}}
**II.3** generally, **magistrate, official,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.29, etc.}}; opposed to ἰδιώτης, {{Refs|“SIG” 672.16 (Delph.)}}; **ruler** of a synagogue, {{NT|NT.Matt.9.18}}; **president** of a club, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.1178.6 (2nd c.AD).}}

759:
** ἄρωμα** [ᾰρ], ατος, τό,
(ἀρόω)
**B** **arable land, corn-land,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 75 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1158, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 304.}}

762:
** ἄσβεστος**, ον,
also η, ον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.123}}:— **unquenchable, inextinguishable,** φλόξ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}} [same place]; **not quenched,** πῦρ ἄ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.67, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 9; κλέος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.584; γέλως 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.599; βοή 11.50; ἐργμάτων ἀκτὶς καλῶν ἄ. αἰεί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).42}}; ἄ. πόρος ὠκεανοῦ ocean's **ceaseless** flow, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 532 (Lyric poetry)}}; πῦρ, of hell, {{NT|NT.Mark.9.43.}}
**II** as _substantive_, ἄσβεστος (i.e. τίτανος), ἡ, **unslaked lime,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.115, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 17, “Eum.” 16; ἄ. κονία 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.25.16.}}
**II.2** a mineral or gem, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 37.146}}. ἀσβεστώδης· **tofus,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}

764:
** ἀσεβ-έω**,
**to be impious, act profanely, commit sacrilege,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.159}}; opposed to ἀδικέω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 367; ἀ. ἐς τὸν νηόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.129, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 490, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.93}}; περὶ τὰ ἱρά, τοὺς θεούς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.139, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 22, etc.; πρὸς τὰ θεῖα [prev. author] “Cyn.” 13.16: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἀ. ἀσέβημα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 910e}}; ἀγγελίας καὶ ἐπιτάξεις παρὰ νόμον ἀ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 941a; περὶ οὗ τὴν ἑορτὴν ἀσεβῶν ἕαλωκε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.227.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _person_, **sin against,** ἢ θεὸν ἢ ξένον τιν᾽ ἀσεβῶν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 271 (Lyric poetry); ἀ. θεούς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.77, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.291c; τὸ ἱαρὸν “IG” 7.2418 (Thebes, 4th c.BC)}}; τὸν Καίσαρα{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1612.23 (3rd c.AD):}} —_passive_, ἀσεβοῦνται οἱ θεοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.7; ἠσεβῆσθαι πρός τινος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 57.9}}; of households, **to be affected with the consequences of sin,** ὅταν τις ἀσεβηθῇ τῶν οἴκων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 877e.}}
**3** _passive_ also of the act, ἐμοὶ ἠσέβηται οὐδὲν περί τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.10; τὰ ἠσεβημένα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.6.}}

766:
** ἀσέλγ-εια**, ἡ,
**licentiousness, wanton violence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 424e, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.13, etc., οἷ προελήλυθ᾽ ἀσελγείας ἅνθρωπος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.9: joined with ὕβρις, [prev. author] 21.1}}; **insolence,** opposed to κολακεία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.42 O.; τῶν δημαγωγῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1304b22: Astrology texts, epithet of certain ζῴδια, Vett. Val. 335.34.}}
**II** **licentiousness,** περὶ τὰς σωματικὰς ἐπιθυμίας{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 36.15.4, etc.}}

767:
** ἄσημος**,
_Doric dialect_ ἄσᾱμος, ον, **without mark** or **token,** ἄ. χρυσός **uncoined** gold, bullion, or plate, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.41}}; ἄ. χρυσίον, ἀργύριον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, 6.8, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 69}}; frequently in Inscrr., opposed to ἐπίσημον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 1.170.6, 2.652 B 22, etc., compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 10}}; also of cattle, **not branded,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 7.3171}}; of persons, **without distinguishing marks** (e. g. οὐλαί), {{Refs|“PGrenf.” 1.27.7, +others}}; ἄ. ὅπλα arms **without device,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1112}}: generally, **shapeless, formless,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.160.}}
**2** later τὸ ἄσημον (i.e. ἀργύριον) **plate, silver,** {{LXX+6th c.AD+|LXX.Job.42.11, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.371 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus)}}; μέταλλα ἀσήμου **silver**-mines, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Geographia” 7.2.17}}: also, = **electron,** alloy of gold and silver, or an imitation thereof, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pseudo-Democritus Alchemista Alch. p.49 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, etc.}}:—_masculine_ ἄσημος, ὁ, {{Refs|“PLeid.X.” 6, +others}}
**II** of sacrifices, oracles, and the like, **unintelligible,** χρηστήρια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92. ; χρησμοί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 662}}; ἄ. ὀργίων μαντεύματε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1013.}}
**III** **leaving no mark, indistinct,**
**III.a** to the hearing, πτερῶν γὰρ ῥοῖβδος οὐκ ἄ. ἦν {{Refs|[prev. work] 1004}}; of sounds and voices, **inarticulate, unintelligible,** ἄσημα φράζειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86; ἄ. κνυζήματα [prev. author] 2.2}}; ἄσημα βοῆς, ={ἄσημος βοή}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1209.}}
**III.b** **without significance, meaningless,** [τοῦ διπλοῦ ὀνόματος] τὸ μὲν ἐκ σημαίνοντος καὶ ἀσήμου{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1457a33, “Rh.” 1405a35}}; ἄσημα τρίζειν, of a mouse, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 108.23; μόριον “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.46; λέξις 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 1164.4.}}
**III.c** to the eye, ἄσημον ἔχειν μυελόν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 652a1: generally, πρὸς τὴν αἴσθησιν -ότερα [prev. author] “Aud.” 802a14.}}
**III.d** generally, **unperceived, unnoticed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1596, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 252}}; ἀσήμων ὑπὲρ ἑρμάτων **hidden, sunken** rocks, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 38.}}
**IV** of persons, cities, etc., **of no mark, obscure, insignificant,** οὐκ ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 849, compare “Ion” 8}}; νὺξ οὐκ ἄ. a night **to be remembered** (being a feast), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.8; τὸ τῆς πατρίδος ἢ τοῦ γένους ἄσημον 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Sto.Herc.” 339.16.}}
**V** _adverb_ **-μως** **without leaving traces,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.1, “Morb.Sacr.” 11; ἀ. πορεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.4}}; ἀ. καὶ κενῶς φθέγγεσθαι **inarticulately,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 250.}}
**V.2** **ignobly,** οὐκ ἀ.{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.52, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.10.4.}}

769:
** ἀσθέν-εια**, ἡ,
**want of strength, weakness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, etc.: in _plural_, ἰσχύες καὶ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 618d}}; especially **feebleness, sickliness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.135; ἀ. τοῦ γήρως 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330e; σωμάτων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.36, etc.}}
**2** **disease, sickness,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.49 (_plural_), “OGI” 244.11 (Daphne, 2nd c.BC), etc.}}; δι᾽ ἀσθένειαν{{NT|NT.Gal.4.13.}}
**3** ἀ. βίου **poverty,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.47, 8.51.}}
**4** in moral sense, **feebleness, weakness,** τῆς ἀνθρωπίνης φύσεως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 854a, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1150b19; τοῦ ἀκροατοῦ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1419a18.—Rare in poetry, as 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 269. -έω}} **to be weak, feeble, sickly,** ἀ. μέλη **to be weak** in limb, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 228; τοὺς ὀφθαλμοὺς ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 209e}}; ἀ. ἀσθένειαν {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 155b: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 274, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.47, NT.Matt.10.8, etc.}}; ἠσθένησε he **fell sick,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.13; ἀσθενέων}}**sick man,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 12 (9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. says that μαλακίζεσθχι is used of women)}}; ἠσθενηκότα{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.13.7.}}
**2** **to be needy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 636}}; ἠσθενηκότες, of those **unable to pay** taxes, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PTeb.” 188 (1st c.BC).}}
**3** with _infinitive_, **to be too weak** to do a thing, **not to be able**.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.15.5; εἰς τὸ θεωρεῖν 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.8.4.}}
**4** **decline,** ἠσθένησεν ἡ ἡμέρα εἰς τὴν ἑσπέραν{{LXX|LXX.Judg.19.9.}}

770:
Included with: ** ἀσθέν-εια**, ἡ,
**want of strength, weakness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, etc.: in _plural_, ἰσχύες καὶ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 618d}}; especially **feebleness, sickliness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.135; ἀ. τοῦ γήρως 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330e; σωμάτων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.36, etc.}}
**2** **disease, sickness,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.49 (_plural_), “OGI” 244.11 (Daphne, 2nd c.BC), etc.}}; δι᾽ ἀσθένειαν{{NT|NT.Gal.4.13.}}
**3** ἀ. βίου **poverty,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.47, 8.51.}}
**4** in moral sense, **feebleness, weakness,** τῆς ἀνθρωπίνης φύσεως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 854a, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1150b19; τοῦ ἀκροατοῦ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1419a18.—Rare in poetry, as 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 269. -έω}} **to be weak, feeble, sickly,** ἀ. μέλη **to be weak** in limb, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 228; τοὺς ὀφθαλμοὺς ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 209e}}; ἀ. ἀσθένειαν {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 155b: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 274, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.47, NT.Matt.10.8, etc.}}; ἠσθένησε he **fell sick,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.13; ἀσθενέων}}**sick man,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 12 (9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. says that μαλακίζεσθχι is used of women)}}; ἠσθενηκότα{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.13.7.}}
**2** **to be needy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 636}}; ἠσθενηκότες, of those **unable to pay** taxes, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PTeb.” 188 (1st c.BC).}}
**3** with _infinitive_, **to be too weak** to do a thing, **not to be able**.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.15.5; εἰς τὸ θεωρεῖν 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.8.4.}}
**4** **decline,** ἠσθένησεν ἡ ἡμέρα εἰς τὴν ἑσπέραν{{LXX|LXX.Judg.19.9.}}

772:
** ἀσθεν-ής**, ές,
**without strength, weak,**
**1** in body, **feeble, sickly,** τοὺς ἀσθενέας τῆς στρατιῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.135, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 12; ἀσθενεῖ χρωτὶ βαίνων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.55, etc.; ὁ παντάπασιν ἀ. τῷ σώματι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.165; ἀ. περὶ τὸν ὀφθαλμόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 4}}; τοὺς ἀσθενεστάτους ἐς τὰς ταλαιπωρίας **least able to bear** hardship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134}}; ἀσθενέστερος πόνον ἐνεγκεῖν **too weak** to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.54}}. _adverb_ ἀσθενῶς, ἴσχειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 659e, compare “OGI” 751.8 (Amblada, 2nd c.BC).}}
**2** in mind, and the like, τὸ ἀ. τῆς γνώμης **the weakness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.61.}}
**3** in power, **weak, feeble,** ἀ. δύναμις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9.ά, compare 1.58; τέχνη δ᾽ ἀνάγκης -εστέρα μακρῷ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 514; πόλιν ἑνὸς -εστέραν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1033; εἰς ὠφέλειαν ἀ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 2.15.}}
**4** in property, **weak, poor,** οἱ χρήμασιν ἀσθενέστεροι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.88: _absolutely_, ὅ τ᾽ ἀ. ὁ πλούσιός τε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 433}}; οἱ ἀσθενέστεροι **the weaker sort,** i.e. **the poor,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.30, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.2.}}
**5** **insignificant,** οὐκ ἀσθενέστατος σοφιστὴς Ἑλλήνων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.95}}; **paltry,** ἀ. σόφισμα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1011}}; of streams, **petty, small,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.25}}; of fluids, **of small specific gravity,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.23}}; ἐς ἀσθενὲς ἔρχεται comes to **nothing,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.120}}. _adverb_ **-νῶς** **feebly, without energy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528b}}; **on slight evidence,** ἀπαγγέλλεσθαι{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus}}**Praef.:** _comparative_ ἀσθενεστέρως, ἐπιθυμεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 255e}} **-έστερον** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 172b}} **-έστερα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141.}}

776:
** ἀσῑτ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **want of food,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1105 (both _plural_).}}
**II** **abstinence from food,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 34, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1180b9.}}
**II.2** **want of appetite,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 7.6.}}

777:
** ἄσῑτ-ος**, ον,
**without food, fasting,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.788, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 324, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 24, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.40, 5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.; ἰχθύς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 29}}. _adverb_ **-ως**{{LXX|“Mantissa Proverbiorum” 1.47: ἀσιτί LXX.Job.24.6.}}
**II** **of forbidden food,** εὐωχία ἄ.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.398 (uncertain reading).}}

778:
** ἀσκέω**,
**work** raw materials, εἴρια, κέρα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.388, 4.110}}; **work curiously, form by art,** [κρητῆρα] Σιδόνες πολυδαίδαλοι εὖ ἤσκησαν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 23.743; ἑρμῖν᾽ ἀσκήσας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.198}}; πτύξασα καὶ ἀσκήσασα χιτῶνα having folded and **smoothed** it,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.439; ἅρμα.. χρυσῷ καὶ ἀργύρῳ εὖ ἤσκηται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.438}}; χορὸν ἤσκησεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 18.592; γόμφοις ἀ. 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 87}}: added in _aorist_ _participle_ to Verbs, [θρόνον] τεύξει ἀσκήσας **elaborately,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.240; [χρυσὸν] βοὸς κέρασιν περίχευεν ἀσκήσας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.437; [ἑανὸν] ἔξυσ᾽ ἀσκήσασα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.179.}}
**2** of personal adornment, **dress out, trick out,** ἀ. τινὰ κόσμῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1; ἐς κάλλος ἀσκεῖ}}**decks herself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1073; δέμας [prev. author] “Tr.” 1023:}} —frequently in _passive_, σκιεροῖς ἠσκημένα γυίοις **furnished with**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 61.4; πέπλοισι Περσικοῖς ἠσκημένη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 182; οὐ χλιδαῖς ἠσκημένον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 452}}; of buildings, παστὰς ἠσκημένη στύλοισι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.169; Παρίῳ λίθῳ ἠσκημένα [prev. author] 3.57: _absolutely_, οἴκημα ἠσκημένον [prev. author] 2.130}}; σῶμα λόγοις ἠσκ. **tricked out** with words only, not real, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1217:}} —_middle_, σῶμ᾽ ὅπλοις ἠσκήσατο **adorned his own** person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1379, cf. Alcaeus161.}}
**3** in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}}, **honour** a divinity, **do** him **reverence,** δαίμον᾽ ἀσκήσω θεραπεύων{{Refs|“P.” 3.109; ἀσκεῖται Θέμις “O.” 8.22.}}
**II** **practise, exercise, train,** especially in Prose and Comedy texts, properly of athletic exercise,
**II.1** with _accusative_ of person or thing, ἀ. τὸν υἱὸν τὸν ἐπιχώριον τρόπον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 47; ἀ. τὰ σώματα εἰς ἰσχύν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.20, compare “Mem.” 1.2.19; ἐχθρὸν ἐφ᾽ ἡμᾶς αὐτοὺς τηλικοῦτον ἠσκήκαμεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.28}}:—_passive_, σώματα εὖ ἠσκημένα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.41; εἰς ἀγῶνα ἄμεινον ἡμῶν ἤσκηνται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.52; ἀσκεῖσθαι λέγειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Demon.” 4; τὴν Κυνικὴν ἄσκησιν [prev. author] “Tox.” 27; λόγοις 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 45.2; ἐν παιδείᾳ [prev. author] 60.2; πρός τι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.54.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ of the thing practised, ἀ. τέχνην, πεντάεθλον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.125, 9.33; λόγους 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 53a, 110; μανθάνειν καὶ ἀ. τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 509e}}; ἀ. παγκράτιον, στάδιον, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 795b, “Thg.” 128e; ἠσκηκέναι μηδεμίαν ἄσκησιν κυριωτέραν τῆς πολεμικῆς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1271b5: _metaphorically_, ἀ. τὴν ἀληθείην, δικαιοσύνην, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.209, 1.96; δίκαια 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 913; ἀρετήν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 853, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407a; κακότητα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1066 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 384; ἀσέβειαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 476; τὰ δίκαια 4th c.BC: Crates Thebanus Poeta Philosophus 12; λαλιάν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 931 (_anapaest meter_): with double _accusative_, ἀ. αὑτόν τε καὶ τοὺς σὺν αὑτῷ τὰ πολεμικά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.10.}}
**II.3** with _infinitive_, ἄσκει τοιαύτη μένειν **practise, endeavour** to remain such, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1024; λέγειν ἠσκηκότες [prev. author] “fragment” 963; εὐσεβεῖν ἠσκηκότα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1067}}; ἀ. γαστρὸς κρείττους εἶναι, τοὺς φίλους ἀγαθὰ ποιεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.45, 5.5.12, compare “Mem.” 2.1.6}}; ἤσκει ἐξομιλεῖν παντοδαποῖς he **made a practice of** associating.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ages.” 11.4.}}
**II.4** _absolutely_, **practise, go into training,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 389c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.29; οἱ ἀσκέοντες}}**those who practise gymnastics,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 9; περὶ τὰς βαναύσους τέχνας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.20.9.}}

779:
** ἀσκός**, ὁ,
**skin, hide,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PFay. 121.9 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}; but usually, **skin made into a bag,** especially **wineskin,** οἶνον.. ἀσκῷ ἐν αἰγείῳ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.247, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.78; ἀσκὸν.. μέλανος οἴνοιο 5.265, 9.196}}; ἀσκὸς βοός, of the **bag** in which Aeolus bottled up the winds, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.19, compare 45, 57}}; ἀσκοὺς καμήλων **skins of** camel's **hide,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.9}}; ἀ. Μαρσύεω **bag made from the skin** of Marsyas, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.26; ἀ. ἀφύσητος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 47; εἴ μοι ἡ δορὰ μὴ εἰς ἀσκὸν τελευτήσει ὥσπερ ἡ Μαρσύου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 285c; ἀσκοῖς καὶ θυλάκοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.23, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26}}; ἀσκοὶ πεφυσαμένοι, of mankind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 246; ἄνθρωποι κενεῆς οἰήσιος ἔμπλεοι ἀ. Timo 11}}; ἀσκός, of the human **skin,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.462.}}
**2** **paunch, belly,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 72}}; in oracular language, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 679, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 3.}}
**3** **bellows,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.28.15, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.456d.}}
**4** **bagpipes,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.459.}}
**5** proverbial, **wineskin,** of a toper, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 19: proverbial, ἀεί ποτ᾽ εὖ μὲν ἀ. εὖ δὲ θύλακος ἅνθρωπός ἐστι 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 85}}; “ἀσκός, πέλεκυς” in a child's game, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 5.5}}; ἀσκὸν δείρειν **flay alive,** hence, **abuse, maltreat,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 442}}:—_passive_, ἀσκὸς δεδάρθαι{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 33.7.}}

782:
** ἀσπάζομαι**,
_Epic dialect_ _aorist_ ἀσπάσσατο{{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 990.9}}:—**welcome kindly, greet,** τινά{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.: frequently with _dative_ modi, δεξιῇ ἠσπάζοντο ἔπεσσί τε μειλιχίοισι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.542; χερσίν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.35, +others; ἁδυπνόῳ φωνᾷ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.25}}; μεγάλως ἠσπάζοντο αὐτόν **received** him **with** great **joy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.122, compare 3.1; παρὰ τὴν πόσιν φιλοφρόνως ἀ. [prev. author] 2.121. δ; εὖ νιν ἀσπάσασθε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 524}}: frequently with no modal word, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 596, etc.}}; especially as the common form on meeting, Στρεψιάδην ἀσπάζομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1145, compare “Pl.” 1042 (see. Scholia), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 273c; αὐτὸν ἠσπάζοντο καὶ ἐδεξιοῦνθ᾽ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 752}}; πόρρωθεν ἀ. **salute** from a distance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 153b}}; πρόσωθεν αὐτὴν ἁγνὸς ὢν ἀ. I **salute** her at a respectful distance, i. e. keep away from her, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 102, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 499a}}; ἀ. ταῖς κώπαις, of the **saluting** of ships, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 76}}; ἀ. τινὰ βασιλέα **to hail** or **salute as** king, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.39: _metaphorically_, ἀ. συμφοράν}} **to bid** the event **welcome,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 587.}}
**b** **take leave of,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1276}}; τὰ ὕστατα ἀ. **take a** last **farewell,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.39.}}
**c** as a formula in closing letters, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Rom.16.22, 23, “BGU” 1079.33 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**2** from the modes of salutation in use, **kiss, embrace,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 607; ἀ. τοῖς στόμασι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 1}}; of dogs, **fawn,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 376a}}; **cling fondly to,** ἴσον σ᾽ ὡς τεκοῦσ᾽ ἀσπάζομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1363, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.2; ἐγὼ ὑμᾶς ἀ. καὶ φιλῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29d: _metaphorically_, φιλεῖν καὶ ἀ. τὸ ἄδικον [prev. author] “Lg.” 689a.}}
**3** of things, **follow eagerly, cleave to,** ἀ. τὸ ὅμοιον, οἶνον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 192a, “R.” 475a, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 11.44}}; of dogs, ἀ. τὰ ἴχνη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.7.}}
**4** ἀ. ὅτι.. **to be glad** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 324.}}
**5** with _infinitive_, **to be ready to**.., εὐωχεῖσθαι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.7, compare 31, “VS” 2.25.4.}}(_active_ ἀσπάζω in letters (cf. I. C), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1158.18 (3rd c.AD), +others, compare ἀσπάζομαι· ἀσπάζω, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

783:
** ἀσπ-ασμός**, ὁ,
**greeting, embrace,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 860 (_plural_)}}; οἱ ἔσχατοι, οἱ τελευταῖοι ἀ., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.4, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.45}}: generally, **salutation,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.Matt.23.7, NT.Mark.12.38, “POxy.” 471.67 (2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.76, Prisc.p.316 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**2** **affection,** opposed to μῖσος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 919e.}}

784:
** ἄσπῐλ-ος**, ον,
literal **stainless**: hence, **faultless, without blemish,** λίθοι{{NT+1st c.AD+|“IG” 2.1054c4, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 6.252 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus), NT.1Tim.6.14, NT.1Pet.1.19, etc.; ἄ. ἀπὸ παντὸς κινδύνου “PMag.Leid.V.” 8.11}}: _comparative_ and _superlative_ variants for {ἀσπίλωτος} in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.167.}}
**II** ἄσπιλος· χειμάρρους {{Refs 1st c.AD+|(1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus), 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

785:
** ἀσπίς**, ίδος, ἡ,
**shield,** εὔκυκλος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.428, +others; κυκλοτερής 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.194; ἀσπίδος κύκλος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 489; ὀμφαλόεσσα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.448, +others}}; opposed to Thracian πέλτη and Persian γέρρον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.6, “Mem.” 3.9.2}}; ἀσπίδα ῥῖψαι, ἀποβαλεῖν, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 28, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 19, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.95}}: to estimate a victory, ἀσπίδας ἔλαβον ὡς διακοσίας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.3: _metaphorically_, οὗτος γὰρ ἡμῖν ἀ. οὐ μικρὰ θράσους 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1437; τὴν ἀ. ἀποβέβληκεν τοῦ βίον 4th c.BC: Nicostratus Philosophus Comedy texts 29, compare 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 62.47.}}
**2** collective, **body of men-at-arms,** ὀκτακισχιλίη ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.30, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 78, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.10.}}
**3** military phrases, ἐπ᾽ ἀσπίδας πέντε καὶ εἴκοσι τάξασθαι to be drawn up twenty-five **deep** or **in file,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.93; στρατιὰν τεταγμένην οὐκ ἐπ᾽ ὀλίγων ἀσπίδων [prev. author] 7.79; ἵστασθαι ἐπὶ τρεῖς ἀσπίδας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 66}}; ἐπὶ μιᾶς ἀσπίδος in single line, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.99}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀσπίδα, παρ᾽ ἀσπίδα (opposed to ἐπὶ δόρυ), **on the left, towards** or **to the left,** because the **shield** was on the left arm, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.6, “An.” 4.3.26; παρ᾽ ἀσπίδος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 624; ἐξ ἀσπίδος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.23.5}}; but παρ᾽ ἀσπίδα, literally, beside **the shield,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.400}}; παρ᾽ ἀ. στῆναι stand in **battle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 250, “Ph.” 1001}}; παρ᾽ ἀ. βεβηκέναι{{Refs|[prev. work] 1073; ἐκπονεῖν [prev. author] “Or.” 653, compare “Hel.” 734; ἐς ἀσπίδ᾽ ἥξειν [prev. author] “Ph.” 1326}}; ἀσπίδας συγκλείειν (compare συγκλείω); ἀσπίδα τίθεσθαι **serve in the ranks,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 756a}}; but θέσθαι τὰς ἀ. pile **shields,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.12}}; ἐπειδὰν ἀ. ψοφῇ when the **shields** ring, i.e. when two bodies of men meet in a charge, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 4.3.29}}; ἀσπίδα ἀναδέξαι, ἆραι, as a signal, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.115, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.27.}}
**4** of a round, flat bowl, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 14.}}
**5** **boss** on a door, {{Refs|“IG” 4.1484.79 (Epid.).}}
**II** **asp, Egyptian cobra, Coluber haié,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.191, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 702, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 158, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.570, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 10.31}}; a play on signff. I and II, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 23.}}
**II.2** **ornament** in this form, {{Refs|“OGI” 90.43 (from Rosetta).}}

786:
** ἄσπονδ-ος**, ον,
**without** σπονδή or **drink-offering**: hence,
**I** of a god, **to whom no drink-offering is poured,** ἄ. θεός, i.e. death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 424.}}
**II** **without a regular truce** (ratified by σπονδαί), ἀνοκωχή{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.32}}; of persons, **without making a truce,** ἄ. ἀπιέναι{{Refs|[prev. author] 3.111, compare 113}}; ἀσπόνδους τοὺς νεκροὺς ἀνελέσθαι take up their dead **without leave asked,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.22}}; τὸ εὐπρεπὲς ἄσπονδον the specious plea **of neutrality,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.37.}}
**II.2** **admitting of no truce, implacable,** ἄσπονδόν τ᾽ Ἄρη (ἀράν codices) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1235 (Pors.); πόλεμος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.262, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.65.6, etc.; ἔχθρα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 30; ἀσπόνδοισι νόμοισιν ἔχθραν συμβάλλειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 905 (Lyric poetry)}}; of persons, **implacable,** {{NT|NT.2Tim.3.3}}. _adverb_ -δως, ἔχειν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus “Fragmenta” 24H.}}

787:
** ἀσσάριον**, τό,
(Latin _assarius_ (i.e. **nummus**)), {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.27, “SIG” 2869.5 (from Calymna), “OGI” 484.9, +others (Pergam., 2nd c.AD), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 13, NT.Matt.10.29.}}
**II** a sort of **valve,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “(Spiritalia) Pneumatica” 1.10.}}

788:
** ἆσσον**
(_Doric dialect_ ἄσσιον _accusative_ to {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1643.32)}}_adverb_ _comparative_ of ἄγχι:— **nearer,** especially of hostile approach, ἆσσον ἴτ᾽{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.567, +others, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 748; τείχεος ἆ. ἴσαν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.4, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1306}}; simply of approach, γυναῖχ᾽ ὁρῶ στείχουσαν ἡμῶν ἆ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 312, compare “El.” 900}}; of a woman, ἥτις ἀνδρῶν ἆ. οὐκ ἐλήλυθεν{{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1306; δίφρον ἆ. ἕλκεται πυρός 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.26: with _dative_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 722}}: with double _comparative_, ἕρποντι μᾶλλον ἆσσον{{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 1210}}: _superlative_ ἄσσιστα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 6; ἄσιστα “IG” 5(2).159.17 (from Tegea), “Michel” 1334 (Elis, 4th c.BC).}}
**II** hence new _comparative_ ἀσσοτέρω, with or without _genitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.506, 17.572}}; later _comparative_ _adjective_ ἀσσότερος, ={ἐγγύτερος}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 878}}: _superlative_ _adverb_ ἀσσοτάτω{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.430 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus): _superlative_}}_adjective_ ἀσσότατος{{Refs|[prev. work] 6.345 ([prev. author]).}}

790:
** ἀστᾰτέω**,
**to be never at rest,** πόλοιο φορὰν.. -έουσαν{{Refs|“App.Anth.” 3.146.4 (from Theon)}}; of the sea, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 17; βλέμμα ἀστατοῦν “Hippiatrica” 3.}}
**2** **to be unsettled, to be a wanderer,** {{NT|NT.1Cor.4.11}}; **to be inconstant,** περὶ τοὺς γάμους{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 116.30.}}

791:
** ἀστεῖος**, α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 73}}: (ἄστυ):— **of the town** (but in the literal sense ἀστικός is used).
**II** **town-bred, polite,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116d}}; opposed to ἄγροικος, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 3; γένοιτ᾽ ἀστεῖος οἰκῶν ἐν πόλει 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus Comedy texts 26}}; **charming,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.34.}}
**II.2** of thoughts and words, **refined, elegant, witty,** διάλεκτον ἀστείαν ὑποθηλυτέραν, opposed to ἀνελεύθερον ὑπαγροικοτέραν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 685; ἀστεῖόν τι λέξαι [prev. author] “Ra.” 901}}; ἀστεῖον λέγεις (where there is a play on the double sense, **witty** and **popular**) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 204; ἀ. καὶ δημωφελεῖς οἱ λόγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 227d; ἀστεῖον εἰπεῖν “Comedy texts Anon.” 248 Mein., compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Axiopistus Poeta Ethicus 1.14; ἀστειοτάτας ἐπινοίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 539}}; of persons, οἱ ἀ. **the wits,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 452d}}; τὰ ἀ. **witty sayings, witticisms,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1411b21, +others}} _adverb_ **-ως**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.4.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.123f, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 13.}}
**II.3** as a general word of praise, of things and persons, **pretty, charming,** βοσκήματε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 811; ἑορτή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 447a; ἀ. καὶ εὐήθης [prev. author] “R.” 349b, compare “Phdr.” 242e, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 13; ἐστὶ γοῦν ἁπλῆ τι;—ἀστεία μὲν οὖν 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 21}}; ἀστεῖόν [ἐστι] ὅτι ἐρυθριᾷς it is **charming** to see you blush, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204c}}; ἀστεῖον πάνυ εἰ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 149.}}
**II.3.b** ironically, ἀ. κέρδος a **pretty** piece of luck, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1064; ἀστεῖος εἶ 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 73.}}
**II.4** of outward appearance, **pretty, graceful,** {{LXX+4th c.BC+|LXX.Exo.2.2, +others; οἱ μικροὶ ἀ. καὶ σύμμετροι, καλοὶ δ᾽ οὔ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1123b7}}; **handsome,** {{LXX|LXX.Josh.3.17 (of Eglon)}}: in Comedy, of **dainty** dishes, κραμβίδιον, κρεΐσκον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 6, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 189.}}
**II.5** **good** of its kind, αἷμα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τροφῆς” 44; ἑλλέβορος 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.3.3; οἶνος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.620d}}; of persons, **good,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.97, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 5}}; ἀστεῖα **good qualities,** opposed to φαῦλα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 114.}}_adverb_ -είως **honourably,** πράττων{{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.2Mac.12.43, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.244.}}

792:
** ἀστήρ**, ὁ,
_genitive_ έρος: _dative_ _plural_ ἀστράσι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.28, 317 (3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus}}; ἄστρασι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|Scholia Ven., 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus)}}:—**star** (see. ἄστρον), ἀστέρ᾽ ὀπωρινῷ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.5; οὔλιος ἀ. 11.62; Σείριος ἀ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 417}}; ἀ. Ἀρκτοῦρος **the chief star** in the constellation, {{Refs|[prev. work] 565}}, etc.; **shooting star** or **meteor,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.75; οἱ διατρέχοντες ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 838; ᾄττοντας ὥσπερ ἀστέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 621b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 341a33, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agis” 11.}}
**2** **flame, light, fire,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1131 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** ἀστὴρ πέτρινος **meteoric** stone, {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 2.13.9.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ of **illustrious** persons, etc., φανερώτατον ἀστέρ᾽ Ἀθήνας{{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1122 (Lyric poetry); Μουσάων ἀστέρα καὶ Χαρίτων “Anthologia Graeca” 7.1.8 (7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus Mess.)}}
**III** **star-fish,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 32, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 548a7, “PA” 681b9, etc.}}
**IV** name of a **bird,** perhaps **goldfinch,** {{Refs|Dionysius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “de Avibus” 3.2.}}
**V** **blue daisy, Aster Amellus,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.66, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.119.}}
**VI** **Samian clay** used as sealing-wax, and in Medicine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 63, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.153, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.178, +others}}
**VII** architectural **ornament,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 4.1484.83 (Epid.), “SIG” 241 B 111 (Delph., 4th c.BC).}}
**VIII** **bandage,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18 (1).823.}}
**VIII.2** name of various **remedies,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 12.761, +others}}
**IX** **birthmark in form of star,** Carcin. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1454b22}}: in Palmistry, a **mark** on the hand, τῷ ύ στοιχείῳ παραπλήσιον{{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 7.238.28}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _stár_- `star', Latin _stella_ (from **stēr-la**), Gothic _stairnō._)

794:
** ἄστοργ-ος**, ον,
**without natural affection,** ἄστοργος ψυχήν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.146; ὥστοργος}}**the heartless one,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.112, compare “Lyric poetry Alex. Adesp.” 6.9; ἄ. γυνή 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.43; ἄ. πρὸς τὰ ἔκγονα 4th-3rd c.BC: Clytus Historicus I, compare “IG” 12(5).14 (from Ios)}}; ἄ. θάνατος **cruel,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 7.662 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus), “IG” 3.1374.}}
**2** **without attraction,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.926f}}:—also ἀστόργης (sic) {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 1.50.}}

796:
** ἀστρᾰπ-ή**, ἡ,
={ἀστεροπή},{στεροπή},**fiash of lightning, lightning,** βροντὴ καὶ ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.86, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.31, etc.; βροντὴ δ᾽ ἐρράγη δι᾽ ἀστραπῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 578, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 68a, “Cra.” 409c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 369b6}}; personified, as subject of painting, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 35.96, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.14}}: frequently in _plural_, **lightnings,** τὰς ἀ. τε καὶ κεραυνίους βολάς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 430; τᾶν πυρφόρων ἀστραπᾶν κράτη νέμων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 201 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **light** of a lamp, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 386, NT.Luke.11.36.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, ἀστραπήν τιν᾽ ὀμμάτων **flashing** of the eyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 474; βλέπων ἀστραπάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 566}}; ἐκτυφλοῦν τιν᾽ ἀστραπὴ [εἰμί] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 195.4, compare 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 6.6.}}

797:
** ἀστράπτω**
(compare στράπτω), _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἀστράπτεσκον{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.86}}: _future_ ἀστράψω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 53, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 33.376}}: _aorist_ ἤστραψα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.595, etc.}}:—**lighten, hurl lightnings,** frequently of omens sent by Zeus, ἀστράπτων ἐπιδέξι᾽{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.353; Κρονίδης ἐνδέξια σήματα φαίνων ἀστράπτει 9.237; ὡς δ᾽ ὅτ᾽ ἂν ἀστράπτῃ πόσις Ἥρης 10.5; ἀστράψας δὲ μάλα μεγάλ᾽ ἔκτυπε 17.595; οὑλύμπιος ἤστραπτεν, ἐβρόντα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 531, compare “V.” 626.}}
**2** _impersonal_, ἀστράπτει **it lightens,** ἤστραψε **it lightened,** οὐρανοῦ δ᾽ ἄπο ἤστραψε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 578, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1392b27.}}
**II** **flash** or **glance like lightning,** πᾶς γὰρ ἀστράπτει χαλινός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1067 (Lyric poetry)}}; κατάχαλκον ἀ. πεδίον **gleams** with brass, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ph.” III; so ἀ. χαλκῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.1}}; of the face, εἶδον τὴν ὄψιν.. ἀστράπτουσαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254b; ἀ. τοῖς ὄμμασι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.15}}; of flowers, ἀνεμωνίδες ἀστράπτουσαι **bright,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.64: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἐξ ὀμμάτων δ᾽ ἤστραπτε.. σέλας (i.e. Τυφών)}} **flashed** flame from his eyes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 358; ἵμερον ἀστράπτουσα κατ᾽ ὄμματος “Anthologia Graeca” 12.161 (3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus), compare 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus [same place]; ἤστραψε γλυκὺ κάλλος “Anthologia Graeca” 12.110 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.2** of persons, **to be brilliant, conspicuous,** ἔν τινι{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.361, 2.23.}}
**III** _transitive_, **consume with lightning,** uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 53.}}
**III.2** **illuminate,** τι {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Musaeus Epicus 276.}}

798:
** ἄστρον**, τό,
mostly in _plural_, **the stars,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.555, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.312, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 458, “Ag.” 4, etc.}}; τοῦ κατ᾽ ἄστρα Ζηνός, ={τοῦ ἐν οὐρανῷ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1106}}; ἄστρων εὐφρόνη, ={εὐφρ. ἀστερόεσσα}, [{{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 19: _sing._, like{ἀστήρ}, frequently of Sirius (in full, σήριον ἄστρον}}] probable reading in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 23.63), 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 39, 40, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.6, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.10.9, +others}}; περὶ τὸ ἄ. in the **dog-days,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.7}}; _poetry_ of the sun, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 411b}}: seldom of **any common star,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).16, Scholia 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 11}}; of the fixed stars, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 290a20}}; ἄστρα πλανητά, opposed to ἀπλανῆ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 38c}}; opposed to ἐνδεδεμένα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 346a2}}; opposed to ἀστέρες, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.21.9}}; ἐπὶ τοῖς ἄστροις at the times of **the stars'** rising or setting, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 10, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 568a18}}; ἄστροις σημαίνεσθαι, τεκμαίρεσθαι, guide oneself by **the stars,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 2.7, 7.48}}; ἄστροις τὸ λοιπὸν ἐκμετρούμενος χθόνα knowing its place only by **the stars,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 795: _metaphorically_, ἐχθροῖς ἄ. ὣς λάμψειν [prev. author] “El.” 66.}}
**II** of something **brilliant, admirable,** Ἀκροκόρινθον Ἑλλάδος ἄ.{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.297 (3rd c.BC: Polystratus Epicureus), compare 9.400 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus), “APl.” 4.295; Σωκρατικῆς σοφίης ἄ. “IG” 3.770 a.}}
